WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:02.370
- My name is Chris Kraus.

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:07.110
I'm currently the chair of the
classics department at Yale.

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:09.380
That said, I'm now going to back off

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:12.360
and introduce you to the introducers,

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.200
the two faculty members who are organizing

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:17.820
the series currently,

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.840
beginning with my
beloved former colleague,

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:21.410
Irene Peirano Garrison,

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:24.190
who is currently the Pope
Professor of Latin at Harvard,

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:25.870
if you can believe it.

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:27.023
So, Irene.

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:31.080
- Good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.720
It's such a pleasure to
see the community gathered

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.290
to hear Shelley Haley today.

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:38.700
Thank you, Chris,

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.260
and also to my co-organizer, Ayesha,

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:44.963
for inviting me back for
this momentous occasion.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:47.530
I want to tell you a little bit

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:50.230
about the background to this lecture.

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:53.980
It was the spring of 2018

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:56.870
when Ayesha and I were asked,

00:00:56.870 --> 00:01:00.600
I believe by Emily
Greenwood at that point,

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:04.900
to take over the organization
of the Parry Lectures

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:08.370
after our colleagues, Chris
Kraus and David Quinn,

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:11.670
had quite a long stint in that position.

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:16.670
And as Ayesha and I
began plotting a strategy

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:19.443
and a course for the Parry Lectures,

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:25.350
it became immediately clear
to us that race was a topic

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:30.490
that was ubiquitous and
bubbling beneath the surface

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:33.550
in so many conversations and encounters

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:36.320
that we were having with
students and colleagues,

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.200
but a topic that at that point

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.960
wasn't present enough
in academic discourse

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:44.160
and frameworks that we were
encountering in our work

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.503
in pre-modern studies, certainly at Yale.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:51.340
How to talk about race from
a historical perspective,

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:54.530
how to draw from critical frameworks

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:57.530
in ways that are sensitive
to the specificity

00:01:57.530 --> 00:01:59.170
of the pre-modern world

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:03.310
and above all, a topic
that is very dear to us,

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:05.700
how to weave scholarly narratives

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:08.780
and perspectives in and out of reflections

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:12.030
on embodied identities
in the here and now,

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:14.290
these all seemed really pressing questions

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:16.210
to us at the time.

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:17.710
We were immediately drawn

00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:21.630
to Shelley Haley's
exemplary investment in all

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:23.960
of these questions from angles that are

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:26.690
at once scholarly, deeply political,

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:29.010
but also deeply personal.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:33.484
And you will all might
immediately recognize

00:02:33.484 --> 00:02:35.840
in particular her trailblazing work

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:40.090
on Fanny Jackson Coppin and
other black women classicists

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:43.390
and other seminal
contributions on translation,

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:46.880
race and the classics that
I'm sure my colleague, Ayesha,

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:48.860
will mentioned momentarily.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:52.900
We initially planned this
lecture for March, 2020,

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:54.780
but as we all know,

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:59.150
back then we were in the
grips of the epidemics

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:02.010
and we were forced to postpone.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:04.690
We could not have predicted a year,

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:06.220
even two years ago,

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:09.390
that the summer of civil unrest

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:13.750
and awakening that we
experienced last year

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:15.350
after the murder of George Floyd

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:18.270
and that of so many
others made the questions

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:19.450
all the more pressing

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:23.830
and avoiding those questions
unconscionable, frankly.

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:25.610
We're grateful to Professor Haley

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:28.230
for accepting the original invitation

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:31.390
and we're so excited for the opportunity

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:34.950
that this unavoidable
postponement provides us

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:37.150
to engage with her groundbreaking work

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:39.750
and learn from her unique personal voice.

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:42.423
So thank you and onto you, Ayesha.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:44.496
- Thank you, Irene,

00:03:44.496 --> 00:03:47.430
and this is one of those
lectures where there is a chain

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:49.420
of introductions, which is,

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:50.400
is no less than what

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:53.370
I think Shelley Haley certainly deserves.

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:56.990
Before I do the the ritual
formality of introduction,

00:03:56.990 --> 00:04:00.630
I do wanna point briefly to an extension

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:03.110
of what Irene began to talk about,

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:04.800
which is the intersection of voice,

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:06.110
critical subjectivity

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:08.070
and the nature of our scholarship.

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:09.067
The Parry Lecture in classics

00:04:09.067 --> 00:04:10.770
and comparative literature speaks

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:12.980
to a very wide range of scholars,

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:14.430
those not just in classics,

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:16.520
but those of us who draw on

00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:18.130
and are adjacent to

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:22.350
and engage variously with
the work in classics,

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:23.330
in comparative literature

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:28.030
and other literary and
humanities disciplines at large.

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:31.470
I wanna begin by noting that
Shelley Haley's work first came

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:34.690
to my attention, not for
her work as a classicist

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:36.370
but because I was teaching a course

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:37.690
in global Shakespeares

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:41.070
and I wanted to find an
essay that would introduce

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:42.820
my students to the challenges

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:45.300
of reading Shakespeare's Cleopatra.

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:48.050
And the essay that I assign regularly now

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:51.447
is Shelley's classic
essay from the early 90s,

00:04:51.447 --> 00:04:54.070
"Black Feminist Thought and the Classics"

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:57.940
an essay that blew open
my students' minds,

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:02.580
not only for its careful
working through of the manner

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:03.930
and mode of scholarship

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:06.210
on a complicated subject like Cleopatra,

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:09.440
but also for its ownership
of her own position

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:11.210
as a scholarly subject,

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:12.370
as a student,

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:16.110
as a member of many complex,
overlapping communities.

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:18.711
And it is that voice that I
think has reached scholars

00:05:18.711 --> 00:05:21.090
across a very wide range of disciplines

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:22.210
and that I certainly,

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:23.370
and I know many others here today,

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:25.420
are excited to hear.

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:27.830
So by way of introduction, I
would actually like to start

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:30.340
with some words that
Shelley herself has written

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:32.340
which I think are particularly crucial

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:33.810
for our own moment today

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:37.070
as we begin this Parry Lecture.

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:38.590
Quoting Patricia Williams,

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:41.017
Shelley Haley writes
in that seminal essay,

00:05:41.017 --> 00:05:42.580
"Black Feminist Thought and the Classics"

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:44.690
and this is Williams,

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:47.260
my attempts to write in my
own voice have placed me

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:49.690
in the center of a
snarl of social tensions

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:51.140
and crossed boundaries.

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:53.730
And reflecting on that
quote, Shelley writes,

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:56.020
think of the possibilities in my case,

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:58.840
feminist classicist and women classicist,

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:01.990
black classicist and
black woman's classicist

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:05.090
and black feminist and
black feminist classicist.

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:07.560
If oxymoronic odds come in degrees,

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:09.640
I would be somewhere near the high end.

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:11.560
How did I come in this location

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:14.123
as a black feminist classicist?

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:16.570
I welcome Shelley Haley here,

00:06:16.570 --> 00:06:18.917
both for her extraordinary career,

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:22.520
she is currently the president

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:24.320
of the Society for Classical Studies

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:27.600
and she is Edward North Chair of Classics

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:30.490
and Professor of Africana
Studies at Hamilton College.

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:31.920
Professor Haley earned her BA

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:34.710
from Syracuse University in 1972.

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:38.690
She received her MA in
1975 and her PhD in 1977,

00:06:38.690 --> 00:06:40.980
both from the University of Michigan.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:42.800
An expert on the figurative Cleopatra,

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:45.610
she has lectured widely on
increasing the representation

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:47.110
of students of color in Latin,

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:49.220
ancient Greek and classics classrooms

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:50.710
and on her research about the role

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:52.930
of a classical education in the lives

00:06:52.930 --> 00:06:57.110
and careers of 19th century
college educated black women.

00:06:57.110 --> 00:06:59.127
Her publications include
Fanny Jackson Coppin's

00:06:59.127 --> 00:07:03.160
"Reminiscences of School Life,
and Hints On Teaching" 1995,

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:04.680
and numerous articles on the role

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:06.290
of women in the ancient world

00:07:06.290 --> 00:07:08.810
and on race and the
discipline of classics.

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:12.330
In 2017, she received
an award for excellence

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:14.110
in teaching at the collegiate level

00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:16.040
from the Society of Classical Studies.

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:18.100
It's with enormous admiration

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:21.910
and a certain amount of modeling

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:24.580
and exemplary awe

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:27.120
that I welcome Shelley Haley
to give the Parry Lecture.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:28.370
Welcome, Professor Haley.

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:31.010
- Thank you so much.

00:07:31.010 --> 00:07:32.498
Thank you.

00:07:32.498 --> 00:07:35.303
It's an absolute honor to be here.

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:39.973
I would like to start first
with a land acknowledgement.

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:46.058
I respectfully acknowledge
the Oneida Nation

00:07:46.058 --> 00:07:47.293
of the Haudenosaunee,

00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:52.340
their elders, both past and present,

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:55.090
as well as future generations.

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:58.590
This acknowledgement
demonstrates solidarity

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:01.500
and commitment to the
beginning of the process

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:06.500
of dismantling the ongoing
legacies of settler colonialism.

00:08:09.009 --> 00:08:12.410
I'd like to thank the departments

00:08:12.410 --> 00:08:15.020
of comparative literature and classics

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:18.400
for inviting me to
deliver the Parry Lecture.

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:23.120
I really am beyond honored to do this

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:26.080
and I wanna give a special
shout out to Ayesha

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:29.170
and Irene for persisting

00:08:31.173 --> 00:08:33.170
with the invitation.

00:08:33.170 --> 00:08:36.090
You both have been wonderful contacts

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:37.680
and great models for

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:42.550
how to treat guests

00:08:42.550 --> 00:08:45.513
that you invite to give a lecture.

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:49.032
Before I begin,

00:08:49.032 --> 00:08:53.460
I wonder if we could just
have a moment of silence

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:56.280
for the victims of gun violence,

00:08:56.280 --> 00:08:58.330
especially Colorado

00:08:58.330 --> 00:09:03.210
and especially the eight
victims in Georgia,

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:05.533
six of whom were Asian women.

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:26.543
Thank you.

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:33.220
One of the most frustrating
questions I have received

00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:35.740
throughout my career is,

00:09:35.740 --> 00:09:39.370
which oppression has hindered
you more in classics,

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:41.253
racism or sexism?

00:09:43.230 --> 00:09:47.650
The prescient work of the
Combahee River Collective

00:09:47.650 --> 00:09:50.450
in their statement and the advent

00:09:50.450 --> 00:09:54.430
of critical race feminist
theory has helped me

00:09:54.430 --> 00:09:56.380
and other black indigenous women

00:09:56.380 --> 00:10:00.570
of color formulate
responses which deconstruct

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:03.350
such questions and reconstruct

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:07.293
the totality of our limited experiences.

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:13.560
One such reconstruction is the
concept of racialized gender.

00:10:14.770 --> 00:10:18.200
In discussions about systemic oppression,

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:23.160
the broader framework of
racialized gender can be specified

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:25.760
as gendered racism

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:29.160
or racialized sexism.

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:33.600
But today I will be working
within the broader concept

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:37.840
of racialized gender when
discussing ancient Hellenic

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:39.593
and Roman societies.

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:43.380
Our reception of race and gender

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:47.850
in the ancient world is
situated within the frameworks

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:51.740
we employ, knowingly or unknowingly,

00:10:51.740 --> 00:10:55.900
to analyze these societies.

00:10:55.900 --> 00:10:58.380
In recent years, feminist scholars

00:10:58.380 --> 00:11:03.020
and scholars of race studies
have abandoned the approaches

00:11:03.020 --> 00:11:07.780
of positivism and turned to
those of social construction

00:11:07.780 --> 00:11:09.463
and intersectionality.

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:13.560
The term intersectionality was coined

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:16.410
by the legal scholar, Kimberle Crenshaw,

00:11:16.410 --> 00:11:21.310
in her 1989 article "The
Marginalizing the Intersection

00:11:21.310 --> 00:11:24.610
of Race and Gender: A
Black Feminist Critique

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:28.940
of Antidiscrimination
Doctrine, Feminist Theory

00:11:28.940 --> 00:11:30.910
and Antiracist Politics."

00:11:30.910 --> 00:11:34.000
I love that title because
it's so Victorian.

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:37.000
You know how Victorians
give really long titles.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:37.833
Anyway.

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:41.810
However, as a concept,

00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:46.810
it is rooted in the earlier
1977 Combahee River Collective

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:50.280
statement authored by Barbara Smith,

00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:53.460
Beverly Smith and Demita Frazier.

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:57.670
The statement was one of
the clearest articulations

00:11:57.670 --> 00:12:01.420
of the simultaneity of
oppressions that women

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:05.590
of African descent, in particular, suffer.

00:12:05.590 --> 00:12:07.790
Since Crenshaw's articulation,

00:12:07.790 --> 00:12:11.960
intersectionality has
spread as an analytical tool

00:12:11.960 --> 00:12:15.020
beyond disciplines and borders.

00:12:15.020 --> 00:12:19.610
Intersectional projects and
methods exist in a wide range

00:12:19.610 --> 00:12:22.840
of fields from its original jurisprudence,

00:12:22.840 --> 00:12:25.490
sociology and education,

00:12:25.490 --> 00:12:29.030
to history, psychology and even business

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:35.460
Very few theories have generated the kind

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:39.090
of interdisciplinary and global engagement

00:12:39.090 --> 00:12:43.878
that marks the intellectual
history of intersectionality.

00:12:43.878 --> 00:12:45.255
Yes, okay.

00:12:45.255 --> 00:12:48.980
Intersectionality is a
critical intervention

00:12:48.980 --> 00:12:52.820
against the acentralism
of critical race theory

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:56.950
and is a core concept of
critical race feminism,

00:12:56.950 --> 00:12:58.343
hereafter CRF.

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:03.980
The anti-acentralism of CRF has,

00:13:03.980 --> 00:13:05.770
according to one scholar,

00:13:05.770 --> 00:13:09.580
quote, provided a critique
of the feminist notion

00:13:09.580 --> 00:13:12.970
that there is an essential female voice

00:13:12.970 --> 00:13:16.743
that is that all women feel
one way on the subject.

00:13:17.740 --> 00:13:22.110
CRF highlights the
situation of women of color

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:27.110
whose lives may not conform to
an essential norm, end quote.

00:13:28.640 --> 00:13:32.235
Increasingly, scholars of
the ancient world are finding

00:13:32.235 --> 00:13:37.235
intersectionality a useful tool
to examine ancient societies

00:13:38.290 --> 00:13:40.780
but in a limited way.

00:13:40.780 --> 00:13:45.780
Often classicists neglect
the core axis of race

00:13:46.420 --> 00:13:49.173
in their application of the concept.

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:53.220
For some time now, the concept

00:13:53.220 --> 00:13:56.240
of social construction has proved fruitful

00:13:56.240 --> 00:13:59.840
in the efforts to uncover
the social constructions

00:13:59.840 --> 00:14:03.883
of race and gender which
operated in ancient societies.

00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:07.100
Adrien Katherine Wing notes

00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:11.710
that social constructions
thesis helps us realize that,

00:14:11.710 --> 00:14:15.810
quote, biological races do not exist,

00:14:15.810 --> 00:14:19.010
as recent science has clearly shown.

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:21.690
There is more genetic difference

00:14:21.690 --> 00:14:26.620
within so-called races than between them.

00:14:26.620 --> 00:14:30.300
Instead, races have been
socially constructed

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:35.180
and the legal system
reifies that construction,

00:14:35.180 --> 00:14:39.900
privileging some races
over others, end quote.

00:14:39.900 --> 00:14:43.240
The same analysis applies to genders

00:14:43.240 --> 00:14:46.283
and the intersection of race and gender.

00:14:47.170 --> 00:14:50.980
A sterling example of the
social construction of race

00:14:50.980 --> 00:14:55.980
in the United States comes
to us from Ta-Nehisi Coates

00:14:56.350 --> 00:15:01.350
and I quote, but race is the
child of racism, not the father

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:07.880
and the process of naming the
people has never been a matter

00:15:08.540 --> 00:15:11.580
of genealogy and physiognomy

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:14.590
as much as one of hierarchy.

00:15:14.590 --> 00:15:19.053
This difference in hue and hair is old,

00:15:20.120 --> 00:15:24.940
but the belief in the
preeminence of hue and hair,

00:15:24.940 --> 00:15:29.230
the belief that these factors
can correctly organize

00:15:29.230 --> 00:15:34.230
a society and that they
signify deeper attributes,

00:15:34.520 --> 00:15:37.010
which are indelible,

00:15:37.010 --> 00:15:39.950
this is the new idea at the heart

00:15:39.950 --> 00:15:44.950
of these new people who have
been brought up hopelessly,

00:15:45.110 --> 00:15:49.413
tragically to believe that they are white,

00:15:50.490 --> 00:15:52.180
end quote.

00:15:52.180 --> 00:15:57.180
While both these modern
approaches can get us closer

00:15:57.399 --> 00:16:01.170
to understanding what Tim
Whitmarsh has described

00:16:01.170 --> 00:16:05.243
as hybridized ancient
Greek and Roman worlds,

00:16:06.470 --> 00:16:11.470
they still are flawed
by exclusive binaries

00:16:11.890 --> 00:16:15.540
that is either/or inherited

00:16:15.540 --> 00:16:18.470
by Western epistemological frameworks

00:16:18.470 --> 00:16:22.463
and arguably first expressed by Aristotle.

00:16:23.720 --> 00:16:28.130
For race, recasting Whitmarsh's concept of

00:16:31.329 --> 00:16:32.613
hybridization

00:16:32.613 --> 00:16:35.800
as creolization is a first step

00:16:35.800 --> 00:16:39.440
in dismantling these binaries.

00:16:39.440 --> 00:16:43.320
Furthermore, such reframing
aids in breaking down

00:16:43.320 --> 00:16:48.320
the influence of modern social
constructs of race and gender

00:16:48.770 --> 00:16:53.510
which undergirds our
reception and perception

00:16:53.510 --> 00:16:56.293
of race and gender in antiquity.

00:16:57.860 --> 00:17:00.630
For gender, the construct goes back

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:04.650
to the 19th century when the
rise of eugenic movements

00:17:04.650 --> 00:17:08.430
and the cult of true
womanhood were the scaffolding

00:17:08.430 --> 00:17:11.943
upon which the gender construct was based.

00:17:14.123 --> 00:17:18.200
The concept of racialized
gender provides, I think,

00:17:18.200 --> 00:17:21.110
a fruitful way of
discussing the intersection

00:17:21.110 --> 00:17:23.820
of race and gender in antiquity.

00:17:23.820 --> 00:17:28.380
Up to now, it has been Marxist feminists

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:31.610
who have applied the
concept to labor movements

00:17:31.610 --> 00:17:35.720
and labor history where
the intersection of race,

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:40.720
gender and class has been
paramount to the discussion.

00:17:41.180 --> 00:17:43.410
However, the broader parameters

00:17:43.410 --> 00:17:48.410
of the concept can apply
trans-historically to antiquity.

00:17:50.390 --> 00:17:53.570
As Eileen Boris states, quote,

00:17:53.570 --> 00:17:57.700
race and gender exist in tandem

00:17:57.700 --> 00:18:02.640
to transform profoundly the
ways that each works alone.

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:06.210
Constructed through
gendered representations,

00:18:06.210 --> 00:18:11.210
race, in turn, reconstructs
gender identities.

00:18:11.370 --> 00:18:16.370
The concept of racialized
gender reflects the interaction.

00:18:18.540 --> 00:18:23.540
Furthermore, Boris shows
that racialized gender aids

00:18:23.810 --> 00:18:27.780
in dismantling the simplistic binary

00:18:27.780 --> 00:18:30.750
that men have race,

00:18:30.750 --> 00:18:32.493
women have a gender,

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:34.203
end quote.

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:40.120
So, how would this very
modern concept work

00:18:40.120 --> 00:18:44.240
when applied retrospectively
to ancient Hellenic

00:18:44.240 --> 00:18:46.700
and Roman societies?

00:18:46.700 --> 00:18:48.320
Let's go on a journey

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:52.160
and examine three case
studies, so to speak,

00:18:52.160 --> 00:18:53.043
Medea,

00:18:54.110 --> 00:18:55.580
Cleopatra

00:18:55.580 --> 00:18:56.830
and Sophonisba.

00:18:57.940 --> 00:19:00.790
This entails taking a broad view

00:19:00.790 --> 00:19:04.870
of race to break out of
the traditional binaries

00:19:04.870 --> 00:19:09.870
and coded language that is using
ethnicity in place of race.

00:19:11.950 --> 00:19:16.610
In these case studies I try
to stress that the perception

00:19:16.610 --> 00:19:21.510
and reception of women
and men is influenced

00:19:21.510 --> 00:19:23.970
by racialized gender.

00:19:23.970 --> 00:19:27.080
I hope to show that the
intersection of race

00:19:27.080 --> 00:19:31.886
and gender in antiquity reflects
the transformational nature

00:19:31.886 --> 00:19:36.560
of racialized gender that Boris described

00:19:36.560 --> 00:19:38.423
in the quote above.

00:19:40.100 --> 00:19:43.020
Before discussing the
details of the intersection

00:19:43.020 --> 00:19:45.830
of race and gender in the ancient world,

00:19:45.830 --> 00:19:49.560
it is important to set out
the operating principles

00:19:49.560 --> 00:19:51.700
of the discussion.

00:19:51.700 --> 00:19:55.430
First and foremost, social constructs

00:19:55.430 --> 00:19:58.690
of race and gender existed in antiquity

00:20:01.000 --> 00:20:02.510
but

00:20:02.510 --> 00:20:07.510
they were not the same as modern times

00:20:07.580 --> 00:20:09.823
but nevertheless, they existed.

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:13.360
However, it cannot be stressed enough

00:20:13.360 --> 00:20:18.270
that the reception of the
ancient constructs of race

00:20:18.270 --> 00:20:23.270
and gender in later times
is profoundly influenced

00:20:23.710 --> 00:20:28.710
by the hierarchical framing
described by Coates and others.

00:20:29.740 --> 00:20:33.520
Pushing against that framing to arrive

00:20:33.520 --> 00:20:36.740
at a more plausible analysis of race

00:20:36.740 --> 00:20:40.820
and gender in antiquity
is the intellectual

00:20:40.820 --> 00:20:44.623
and moral imperative of this lecture.

00:20:46.880 --> 00:20:49.900
Ancient formulations of race and gender,

00:20:49.900 --> 00:20:52.590
like their modern counterparts,

00:20:52.590 --> 00:20:56.970
were not static, nor were they immutable.

00:20:56.970 --> 00:20:59.728
Jonathan Hall notes this in his work on

00:20:59.728 --> 00:21:02.730
what he calls ethnic groups,

00:21:02.730 --> 00:21:06.720
I would call them races but
he calls them ethnic groups,

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:09.240
in Hellenic society.

00:21:09.240 --> 00:21:14.240
Quote, ethnic groups are
not static or monolithic,

00:21:14.240 --> 00:21:16.800
but dynamic and fluid.

00:21:16.800 --> 00:21:19.840
Their boundaries are permeable to a degree

00:21:19.840 --> 00:21:23.930
and they may be subject to
processes of assimilation

00:21:23.930 --> 00:21:27.260
and differentiation, end quote.

00:21:27.260 --> 00:21:31.449
While we can argue that
there were relatively uniform

00:21:31.449 --> 00:21:35.750
Roman constructs of race and gender,

00:21:35.750 --> 00:21:39.840
we cannot say that there
were monolithic constructs

00:21:39.840 --> 00:21:42.870
in ancient Hellenic societies.

00:21:42.870 --> 00:21:47.550
These were tied closely to
various demographic groups

00:21:47.550 --> 00:21:51.240
in different city states
around the Aegean.

00:21:51.240 --> 00:21:53.910
So as far as possible,

00:21:53.910 --> 00:21:57.080
I'm going to refer to the city states by

00:22:01.520 --> 00:22:02.353
name.

00:22:03.470 --> 00:22:07.940
The Hellenic peoples enshrined
different racial categories

00:22:07.940 --> 00:22:10.200
in their mythic foundations.

00:22:10.200 --> 00:22:13.020
In this regard, the narratives concerning

00:22:13.020 --> 00:22:17.850
racial genealogies are forms
of social construction,

00:22:17.850 --> 00:22:22.850
as ancient societies strived
to explain the racial

00:22:22.970 --> 00:22:26.993
differences through a shared ancestry.

00:22:28.010 --> 00:22:32.750
In the myths surrounding the
genesis of Hellenic peoples,

00:22:32.750 --> 00:22:37.750
the ancestors of the three
major racial groups are depicted

00:22:38.120 --> 00:22:41.373
as descendants of Zeus or Helen.

00:22:42.874 --> 00:22:43.810
Aeolus,

00:22:43.810 --> 00:22:45.210
Doris

00:22:45.210 --> 00:22:46.830
and Ion

00:22:46.830 --> 00:22:49.100
became the [Indistinct] founders

00:22:49.100 --> 00:22:54.100
of the Aeolian, Dorian and Ionian races.

00:22:54.280 --> 00:22:59.280
Each race also shared
linguistic and cultural traits.

00:22:59.440 --> 00:23:04.050
It is in the latter sphere
that the intersection of race

00:23:04.050 --> 00:23:07.190
and gender is clear.

00:23:07.190 --> 00:23:10.833
The Ionian people, best
represented by Athens,

00:23:11.670 --> 00:23:16.670
constructed gender and gender
relations along rigid lines,

00:23:16.700 --> 00:23:20.450
especially after the legislation of Solon

00:23:20.450 --> 00:23:22.047
in the sixth century BCE.

00:23:24.590 --> 00:23:27.530
Given the importance of racialized gender

00:23:27.530 --> 00:23:31.420
for the construction of the
identity for Hellenic peoples,

00:23:31.420 --> 00:23:35.050
it is not surprising
to find it illustrated

00:23:35.050 --> 00:23:38.733
in cultural production,
especially literature.

00:23:39.570 --> 00:23:41.473
This brings us to Medea,

00:23:42.660 --> 00:23:44.190
a mythic character

00:23:44.190 --> 00:23:49.070
who has become epitome
of a racialized gender.

00:23:49.070 --> 00:23:54.070
She is best known to us from
Euripides' tragedy, "Medea"

00:23:54.790 --> 00:23:57.680
and nearly all the receptions of that play

00:23:57.680 --> 00:24:01.090
from Seneca to Toni Morrison focus

00:24:01.090 --> 00:24:05.103
on the trope of the mother
who kills her own children.

00:24:06.870 --> 00:24:09.670
But what did Euripides know?

00:24:09.670 --> 00:24:13.230
He undoubtedly knew Medea as the daughter

00:24:13.230 --> 00:24:16.370
of Aeetes of Colchiss,

00:24:16.370 --> 00:24:18.420
as the niece of Circe

00:24:19.460 --> 00:24:23.910
and so the granddaughter of Helios.

00:24:23.910 --> 00:24:28.190
He was familiar with the
Corinthiaca, now lost,

00:24:28.190 --> 00:24:30.800
an epic poem which described the death

00:24:30.800 --> 00:24:34.053
of Medea's children in the Temple of Hera.

00:24:35.280 --> 00:24:39.580
Other versions of the story
portray the Corinthians

00:24:39.580 --> 00:24:43.260
as the murderers of the children.

00:24:43.260 --> 00:24:46.910
As retribution, the
Corinthians were forced

00:24:46.910 --> 00:24:51.570
to establish a hero cult for the children.

00:24:51.570 --> 00:24:54.011
In the fifth century BCE,

00:24:54.011 --> 00:24:58.330
the stain of infanticide
still haunted the Corinthians

00:24:58.330 --> 00:25:03.330
and so they commissioned
Euripides to revision the myth.

00:25:04.880 --> 00:25:06.920
In Euripides' hands,

00:25:06.920 --> 00:25:11.003
Medea becomes the foreign
woman extraordinaire,

00:25:11.890 --> 00:25:16.170
yet the description of Medea as foreign

00:25:17.420 --> 00:25:21.713
had a way of masking
her racialized gender.

00:25:22.820 --> 00:25:26.530
So beyond divine genealogy,

00:25:26.530 --> 00:25:31.530
how did Euripides read
Medea's racial ancestry?

00:25:33.700 --> 00:25:37.420
Herodotus informs us that
Colchis was colonized

00:25:37.420 --> 00:25:40.910
by the Egyptian pharaoh, Sesostris,

00:25:40.910 --> 00:25:43.053
probably Sesostris II.

00:25:44.520 --> 00:25:48.950
Herodotus states and I quote,
the Egyptians did, however,

00:25:48.950 --> 00:25:53.950
say that they thought the
original Colchians were men

00:25:54.500 --> 00:25:57.510
from Sesostris's army.

00:25:57.510 --> 00:26:01.290
My own idea on the subject was based first

00:26:01.290 --> 00:26:05.730
on the fact that they had
black skins and wooly hair,

00:26:05.730 --> 00:26:10.730
not that amounts to much as
other nations have the same.

00:26:11.840 --> 00:26:15.233
And secondly, and more especially,

00:26:16.640 --> 00:26:19.070
on the fact that the Colchians,

00:26:19.070 --> 00:26:24.070
the Egyptians and the
Ethiopians are the only races

00:26:24.770 --> 00:26:29.683
which from ancient times
practiced circumcision,

00:26:30.870 --> 00:26:31.703
end quote.

00:26:32.860 --> 00:26:36.880
Now this information
from Herodotus comes in,

00:26:36.880 --> 00:26:40.770
within the context of the
construction of peoples

00:26:40.770 --> 00:26:44.280
other than Hellenic as a barbarian,

00:26:44.280 --> 00:26:47.010
that's Herodotus' word.

00:26:47.010 --> 00:26:49.400
With this knowledge in hand,

00:26:49.400 --> 00:26:53.450
Euripides then establishes
Medea as racially

00:26:53.450 --> 00:26:56.200
and culturally other

00:26:56.200 --> 00:27:01.200
and sets her up as a foil to
Jason's racialized gender,

00:27:02.680 --> 00:27:06.930
one that is constructed as more civilized

00:27:06.930 --> 00:27:08.693
and hence superior.

00:27:09.830 --> 00:27:14.830
Euripides' revision has
had such sustaining power

00:27:15.530 --> 00:27:19.733
because it resonated
with later internalized

00:27:20.810 --> 00:27:24.360
negative perceptions of
the racialized gender,

00:27:24.360 --> 00:27:27.753
particularly of women of African descent.

00:27:28.720 --> 00:27:32.590
In Jason's soliloquy,
after the infanticide,

00:27:32.590 --> 00:27:34.890
he declares that, quote,

00:27:34.890 --> 00:27:38.560
no woman born in a Hellenic society would

00:27:38.560 --> 00:27:42.263
ever have gone through with such a crime.

00:27:44.100 --> 00:27:46.030
And later in the same passage,

00:27:46.030 --> 00:27:50.090
he invokes animal and monster imagery.

00:27:50.090 --> 00:27:53.470
A lioness, not a woman, you.

00:27:53.470 --> 00:27:56.773
More cruel in nature
than the Etruscan Scylla.

00:27:58.810 --> 00:28:03.010
Flash forward to the 19th
through 21st centuries,

00:28:03.010 --> 00:28:07.050
we black women have been masculinized,

00:28:07.050 --> 00:28:09.000
declared unfit mothers

00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:11.800
and portrayed as not just angry,

00:28:11.800 --> 00:28:13.363
but full of rage.

00:28:14.660 --> 00:28:19.660
Yet what gets largely ignored
is that Jason cannot succeed

00:28:20.750 --> 00:28:24.230
without Medea's knowledge and skills.

00:28:24.230 --> 00:28:27.830
And even after the murder of her children,

00:28:27.830 --> 00:28:32.830
she, not the Corinthians,
buries her children

00:28:33.270 --> 00:28:35.883
and establishes a cult in their honor.

00:28:37.760 --> 00:28:41.130
It is notable that in Euripides' "Medea"

00:28:41.130 --> 00:28:44.020
it is the chorus of Corinthian women

00:28:44.020 --> 00:28:48.950
who express the injustice
of women's position

00:28:48.950 --> 00:28:51.023
in Euripides' Athens.

00:28:52.310 --> 00:28:57.310
These female members of the
Aeolian demographic demonstrate

00:28:57.550 --> 00:28:59.890
a different construction of gender

00:28:59.890 --> 00:29:03.393
when they reflect on the
historical treatment of women.

00:29:04.540 --> 00:29:09.540
It is to the thoughts of
men which are deceitful,

00:29:10.100 --> 00:29:12.573
their pledges which are loose.

00:29:13.540 --> 00:29:16.480
The stories of women shall
not turn our condition

00:29:16.480 --> 00:29:18.610
into a fair one

00:29:18.610 --> 00:29:23.090
so that our lives shall
achieve proper glory.

00:29:23.090 --> 00:29:27.390
New value is coming for female kind,

00:29:27.390 --> 00:29:31.780
no longer shall slanders
pollute our story.

00:29:31.780 --> 00:29:36.720
The poems of long ago bards
shall no more portray us

00:29:36.720 --> 00:29:40.053
as fickle, untrustworthy friends,

00:29:41.000 --> 00:29:46.000
biased because Apollo
forbore to implant his lyrics

00:29:47.120 --> 00:29:49.230
in feminine minds.

00:29:49.230 --> 00:29:52.890
I tried to do that as a
rap, but it wasn't working,

00:29:52.890 --> 00:29:55.549
so, my apologies.

00:29:55.549 --> 00:30:00.350
Despite this plea for
equality of representation

00:30:00.350 --> 00:30:02.870
in literature and history,

00:30:02.870 --> 00:30:05.080
the "Medea", the play,

00:30:05.080 --> 00:30:10.080
concretizes the binary
between foreign born

00:30:10.180 --> 00:30:14.833
and native women in the
Hellenic male consciousness.

00:30:16.180 --> 00:30:18.580
Now before discussing racialized gender

00:30:18.580 --> 00:30:21.450
as constructed by the Romans,

00:30:21.450 --> 00:30:24.513
we need to establish the gender norm.

00:30:25.510 --> 00:30:27.480
Just as in the United States,

00:30:27.480 --> 00:30:32.350
when we say African American
men, we mean black men.

00:30:32.350 --> 00:30:36.440
I'm sorry, when we say African
Americans, we men black men.

00:30:36.440 --> 00:30:39.323
And when we say Americans,
we mean white men.

00:30:41.110 --> 00:30:45.260
So for the Romans, Romani meant Roman men

00:30:46.480 --> 00:30:49.730
and Roman masculinity racialized gender

00:30:49.730 --> 00:30:53.880
in and of itself was the norm in the texts

00:30:53.880 --> 00:30:55.980
of Roman literature.

00:30:55.980 --> 00:31:00.190
Roman society was
patriarchal and endocentric.

00:31:00.190 --> 00:31:03.320
The authorship of the texts we'll look at,

00:31:03.320 --> 00:31:06.653
being all male, reflects that.

00:31:07.740 --> 00:31:11.680
Gender difference is
filtered through a male lens

00:31:11.680 --> 00:31:16.510
and that lens is the framework
for gender difference.

00:31:16.510 --> 00:31:19.098
It's like a circular argument.

00:31:19.098 --> 00:31:23.770
Racialized gender was central
to the founding of Rome

00:31:23.770 --> 00:31:26.500
from whatever starting .1 case,

00:31:26.500 --> 00:31:29.850
whether the legends of Aeneas or those

00:31:29.850 --> 00:31:32.783
of his descendants, Romulus and Remus,

00:31:34.007 --> 00:31:38.270
the intersection of race and
gender formed the foundation

00:31:38.270 --> 00:31:39.973
of Rome's origins.

00:31:41.210 --> 00:31:44.220
But Julio-Claudians, the founding dynasty

00:31:44.220 --> 00:31:45.810
of the Roman Empire,

00:31:45.810 --> 00:31:49.940
claimed ancestry of the god, Venus.

00:31:49.940 --> 00:31:54.240
Furthermore, the Romans
constructed a national identity

00:31:54.240 --> 00:31:57.010
upon the Trojan hero, Aeneas.

00:31:57.010 --> 00:31:58.663
As a son of Venus,

00:32:01.080 --> 00:32:06.080
he imparted to the Romans a
shared ancestry with Venus.

00:32:07.800 --> 00:32:10.320
Any discussion of racialized gender

00:32:10.320 --> 00:32:12.450
as it appears in the literary production

00:32:12.450 --> 00:32:15.010
of the Augustan Age would be remiss

00:32:15.010 --> 00:32:17.290
without explicit attention paid

00:32:17.290 --> 00:32:21.040
to Cleopatra VII of Egypt.

00:32:21.040 --> 00:32:24.610
Cleopatra was the inspiration
for Virgil's reception

00:32:24.610 --> 00:32:26.880
of Dido in the Aeneid

00:32:26.880 --> 00:32:30.230
and as we will see for Livy's retelling

00:32:30.230 --> 00:32:32.640
of the story of Sophonisba.

00:32:33.900 --> 00:32:36.530
The intersection of Cleopatra's race

00:32:36.530 --> 00:32:40.740
and gender was paramount to the treatment

00:32:40.740 --> 00:32:44.090
that the historical Cleopatra received

00:32:44.090 --> 00:32:45.620
from prominent Romans,

00:32:45.620 --> 00:32:50.263
such as Cicero and Augustus, as Octavian.

00:32:51.520 --> 00:32:53.730
Consequently, it should be no surprise

00:32:53.730 --> 00:32:56.000
that this intersection weighed heavily

00:32:56.000 --> 00:32:59.100
on the literary imagination of the writers

00:32:59.100 --> 00:33:00.703
of the Augustan period.

00:33:02.190 --> 00:33:06.540
After all, Cleopatra embodies
the racialized gender

00:33:06.540 --> 00:33:11.540
which pervades all the fears
of the Roman ruling class men,

00:33:12.610 --> 00:33:17.280
a foreign woman with political
power in a geopolitical area

00:33:17.280 --> 00:33:21.860
which historically produced
Rome's most tenacious

00:33:21.860 --> 00:33:26.683
and feared rivals, Hannibal and Cleopatra.

00:33:27.850 --> 00:33:32.770
So, how does Cleopatra
fare in the Rome, Roman

00:33:32.770 --> 00:33:37.770
and Rome-identified authors who
dominate the source material

00:33:38.620 --> 00:33:41.670
and who would write their accounts one

00:33:41.670 --> 00:33:45.213
to two centuries after she died?

00:33:48.210 --> 00:33:50.750
Let's look at the two major ones,

00:33:50.750 --> 00:33:53.890
Plutarch and Cassius Dio,

00:33:53.890 --> 00:33:56.410
both of whom wrote their works in Greek

00:33:56.410 --> 00:33:59.010
and both of whom embed their discussions

00:33:59.010 --> 00:34:03.100
of Cleopatra within the
life stories of men.

00:34:03.100 --> 00:34:08.100
Plutarch especially capitalizes
on Cleopatra's racialized

00:34:08.270 --> 00:34:11.670
