the property of Rome,

00:52:09.677 --> 00:52:13.200
Masinissa sent poison to his wife.

00:52:13.200 --> 00:52:16.303
In heroic fashion, she drank it and died.

00:52:17.950 --> 00:52:20.426
Now you probably

00:52:20.426 --> 00:52:22.680
realize this already

00:52:22.680 --> 00:52:26.050
but the story is much more complex

00:52:26.050 --> 00:52:29.400
and nuanced than even

00:52:29.400 --> 00:52:34.130
that kind of complicated
summary can relate.

00:52:34.130 --> 00:52:38.590
For instance, each of the two
significant ancient sources

00:52:38.590 --> 00:52:43.293
for the story of Sophonisba
relies on racialized gender,

00:52:43.293 --> 00:52:46.940
gender stereotypes in the characterization

00:52:46.940 --> 00:52:50.500
of Sophonisba, Masinissa and Syphax.

00:52:50.500 --> 00:52:55.180
For instance, Polybius relates
that Scipio found it likely

00:52:55.180 --> 00:52:59.090
that Syphax was bored by his young bride,

00:52:59.090 --> 00:53:00.920
who he never names,

00:53:00.920 --> 00:53:04.630
and hence by his alliance
with the Carthaginians.

00:53:04.630 --> 00:53:08.020
The reason Polybius attributes to Scipio

00:53:08.020 --> 00:53:11.683
for thinking this is
what's important here.

00:53:13.268 --> 00:53:17.780
For Polybius says it was
because, and this is a quote,

00:53:17.780 --> 00:53:22.780
of the natural fickleness of the Numidians

00:53:22.790 --> 00:53:26.913
and their perfidy towards gods and men.

00:53:32.845 --> 00:53:36.970
Polybius does give a hint
of Sophonisba's charming

00:53:36.970 --> 00:53:40.090
and persuasive personality.

00:53:40.090 --> 00:53:43.073
In this section, Sophonisba is begging

00:53:43.073 --> 00:53:46.690
Syphax not to desert the Carthaginians

00:53:46.690 --> 00:53:49.423
and she is moved by his injuries.

00:53:50.620 --> 00:53:55.030
Livy is the source who spends
the most time on Sophonisba.

00:53:55.030 --> 00:53:59.460
We learn from him that
Sophonisba was a maid

00:53:59.460 --> 00:54:01.220
of marriageable age

00:54:02.963 --> 00:54:07.210
and Livy describes how
Hasdrubal took advantage

00:54:07.210 --> 00:54:12.210
of her beauty and charm
and of Syphax's passion

00:54:12.640 --> 00:54:16.250
to bind the Numidian leader more closely

00:54:16.250 --> 00:54:18.093
to the Carthaginian cause.

00:54:20.220 --> 00:54:25.220
Livy's portrayal of Sophonisba
is taken up when, again,

00:54:25.940 --> 00:54:29.583
when Masinissa captures Cirta.

00:54:31.210 --> 00:54:34.260
Masinissa is spotted by Sophonisba,

00:54:34.260 --> 00:54:36.810
now named by Livy,

00:54:36.810 --> 00:54:41.263
who begs the Numidian leader
to save her from the Romans.

00:54:42.140 --> 00:54:44.720
In the act of naming her,

00:54:44.720 --> 00:54:48.260
Livy give Sophonisba not only agency,

00:54:48.260 --> 00:54:50.350
but also accountability.

00:54:50.350 --> 00:54:54.890
She is no longer the
dutiful, nubilous virgo,

00:54:54.890 --> 00:54:57.130
doing her father's bidding.

00:54:57.130 --> 00:55:02.130
She now has the potential
to act in her own name

00:55:02.920 --> 00:55:04.723
and on her own behalf.

00:55:06.140 --> 00:55:11.140
Captivated, Masinissa agrees to help her

00:55:11.320 --> 00:55:13.863
and impetuously marries her.

00:55:14.890 --> 00:55:17.310
None of this is pleasing for Scipio

00:55:17.310 --> 00:55:20.730
who pedantically demonstrates to Masinissa

00:55:20.730 --> 00:55:23.880
that Sophonisba is the property of Rome

00:55:23.880 --> 00:55:27.093
and he must turn her over to him.

00:55:28.516 --> 00:55:30.410
Masinissa, like the good student

00:55:30.410 --> 00:55:33.020
of Roman ideals that he is,

00:55:33.020 --> 00:55:37.510
goes off to consider the
advice and admonition of Scipio

00:55:37.510 --> 00:55:41.900
and resolves to send
Sophonisba a cup of poison

00:55:41.900 --> 00:55:43.823
which she bravely accepts.

00:55:45.520 --> 00:55:48.300
All of the later sources give notice

00:55:48.300 --> 00:55:51.240
of Sophonisba's beauty and education,

00:55:51.240 --> 00:55:55.350
noting that she was well
versed in grammar and music.

00:55:55.350 --> 00:55:58.690
These authors go on to
say that she was clever,

00:55:58.690 --> 00:56:02.930
ingratiating and altogether so charming

00:56:03.820 --> 00:56:08.820
that the mere sight of her
or the sound of her voice,

00:56:10.830 --> 00:56:12.163
who does that sound like?

00:56:13.130 --> 00:56:16.220
Sufficed to vanquish everyone,

00:56:16.220 --> 00:56:18.550
even the most indifferent.

00:56:18.550 --> 00:56:19.950
These later sources,

00:56:19.950 --> 00:56:23.560
no doubt because of their distance

00:56:23.560 --> 00:56:26.900
from the events of the
late Roman Republic,

00:56:26.900 --> 00:56:31.460
make the conflation with
Cleopatra more explicit

00:56:31.460 --> 00:56:33.470
than Livy needs to.

00:56:33.470 --> 00:56:36.800
Nevertheless, the similarity
between Sophonisba's

00:56:37.680 --> 00:56:42.680
and Cleopatra's racialized
gender is particularly striking.

00:56:44.620 --> 00:56:46.720
Whereas the later sources seem

00:56:46.720 --> 00:56:49.890
to be telling a touching story,

00:56:49.890 --> 00:56:52.340
Livy's agenda is very different,

00:56:52.340 --> 00:56:56.840
reestablishing the Roman
moral fabric of old,

00:56:56.840 --> 00:56:59.923
one which has become badly frayed.

00:57:01.440 --> 00:57:05.280
His main vehicle for this
is racialized gender.

00:57:05.280 --> 00:57:09.330
As a result, each of
the male characters has

00:57:09.330 --> 00:57:14.330
his own political and or
moral crisis occurring

00:57:14.510 --> 00:57:17.450
and each crisis gets resolved

00:57:17.450 --> 00:57:22.450
by reasserting the
normative racialized gender.

00:57:22.870 --> 00:57:27.150
For instance, earlier in book 29,

00:57:27.150 --> 00:57:31.930
Livy describes Scipio's crisis surrounding

00:57:31.930 --> 00:57:35.903
the subjugated city of
Locri in Magna Graecia.

00:57:36.870 --> 00:57:40.120
There had been a senatorial investigation,

00:57:40.120 --> 00:57:42.040
it kinda sounds like today,

00:57:42.040 --> 00:57:43.590
you know, every time something goes wrong,

00:57:43.590 --> 00:57:46.880
there's a senatorial investigation,

00:57:46.880 --> 00:57:49.780
into the complaints of the Locrians

00:57:49.780 --> 00:57:52.290
and Scipio's role in them.

00:57:52.290 --> 00:57:55.420
Livy recounts the following charges,

00:57:55.420 --> 00:57:59.043
revealed during the
Senate's deliberations.

00:58:00.460 --> 00:58:04.220
Much was also said against the
commander in chief himself,

00:58:04.220 --> 00:58:05.123
this is Scipio.

00:58:06.980 --> 00:58:10.170
His dress and bearing were unRoman,

00:58:11.800 --> 00:58:14.367
if not and not even soldierly.

00:58:15.340 --> 00:58:17.960
He strolled about the gymnasium

00:58:17.960 --> 00:58:21.940
in a Greek mantle and sandals

00:58:21.940 --> 00:58:25.250
and wasted his time over, on my God, books

00:58:26.240 --> 00:58:27.823
and physical exercise.

00:58:29.540 --> 00:58:31.843
Sorry for the editorial insert there.

00:58:33.460 --> 00:58:36.583
The discipline of the whole
army had gone to the dogs,

00:58:37.650 --> 00:58:42.400
so that it was more of
a menace to its friends

00:58:42.400 --> 00:58:43.923
than its enemies.

00:58:45.940 --> 00:58:50.900
Now while many or even most
of these charges were untrue,

00:58:50.900 --> 00:58:54.150
Scipio does have an image problem

00:58:54.150 --> 00:58:57.300
and he must reclaim
both his moral position

00:58:57.300 --> 00:59:00.810
as the exemplar of the ideal Roman,

00:59:00.810 --> 00:59:03.100
of ideal Roman masculinity,

00:59:03.100 --> 00:59:06.380
as well as his political reputation

00:59:06.380 --> 00:59:08.223
as a military commander.

00:59:09.080 --> 00:59:14.080
Masinissa and Sophonisba
are the tools to his success

00:59:14.140 --> 00:59:15.920
in this reclaiming.

00:59:15.920 --> 00:59:18.260
When Masinissa conquers Cirta

00:59:18.260 --> 00:59:20.110
and defeats Syphax,

00:59:20.110 --> 00:59:24.590
he is an ally of Scipio
and becomes his proxy

00:59:24.590 --> 00:59:28.030
in terms not only in military success

00:59:28.030 --> 00:59:31.260
but also Roman racialized gender.

00:59:31.260 --> 00:59:34.090
However, livy makes very clear

00:59:34.090 --> 00:59:38.350
that Masinissa is racially different

00:59:38.350 --> 00:59:40.723
to the Romans he is emulating.

00:59:41.790 --> 00:59:45.940
Sophonisba is a vehicle
through which Livy reminds us

00:59:45.940 --> 00:59:50.483
that our Numidian hero
is a non-Roman hero.

00:59:51.410 --> 00:59:55.590
The beautiful Carthaginian
women will seduce him

00:59:55.590 --> 00:59:57.930
and cause him to slip back

00:59:57.930 --> 01:00:02.300
into his natural racialized gender

01:00:02.300 --> 01:00:04.943
and stereotype of the Numidian.

01:00:06.880 --> 01:00:08.623
Let's look at what happens.

01:00:10.380 --> 01:00:13.320
When Livy first mentions Sophonisba.

01:00:13.320 --> 01:00:16.530
it is clear that Hasdrubal, her father,

01:00:16.530 --> 01:00:18.610
is the architect of the scheme

01:00:18.610 --> 01:00:21.770
to win over Syphax from the Romans.

01:00:21.770 --> 01:00:25.373
Sophonisba is a pawn in a political move.

01:00:27.123 --> 01:00:30.640
Hasdrubal is relying on
his daughter's beauty

01:00:30.640 --> 01:00:34.320
and Syphax's passionate nature.

01:00:34.320 --> 01:00:39.320
However, what Sophonisba is
the agent of her own destiny

01:00:39.690 --> 01:00:42.930
and comes out to greet Masinissa,

01:00:42.930 --> 01:00:46.790
Livy gives her a speech that demonstrates

01:00:46.790 --> 01:00:47.913
that she has the,

01:00:49.660 --> 01:00:50.493
sorry,

01:00:50.493 --> 01:00:54.720
persuasive power to
obtain whatever she wants,

01:00:54.720 --> 01:00:58.860
as well as the racial rupture caused

01:00:58.860 --> 01:01:02.263
by the Roman invasion of Africa.

01:01:05.380 --> 01:01:08.343
This is Sophonisba according to Livy,

01:01:09.930 --> 01:01:14.930
if I had been nothing other
than the wife of Syphax,

01:01:14.950 --> 01:01:18.150
I would have preferred to trust the honor

01:01:18.150 --> 01:01:22.510
of a Numidian and one born like me

01:01:22.510 --> 01:01:24.280
in the same Africa

01:01:25.250 --> 01:01:28.943
than that of an alien, an outsider.

01:01:30.290 --> 01:01:32.280
What a daughter of Carthage,

01:01:32.280 --> 01:01:35.850
not to mention a daughter of Hasdrubal,

01:01:35.850 --> 01:01:39.010
has to fear from a Roman,

01:01:39.010 --> 01:01:41.013
you do understand.

01:01:43.000 --> 01:01:45.680
If you are able of nothing else,

01:01:45.680 --> 01:01:50.370
then I beg and beseech you
to save me through death

01:01:51.410 --> 01:01:53.193
from the Romans' will.

01:01:55.240 --> 01:01:59.040
Livy overshadows Sophonisba's appeal

01:01:59.040 --> 01:02:03.590
to Masinissa's race
loyalty by again repeating

01:02:03.590 --> 01:02:04.423
the racial.

01:02:09.780 --> 01:02:14.780
As he, Livy, relates the scene
after Sophonisba's speech,

01:02:15.850 --> 01:02:18.570
he describes her as, quote,

01:02:18.570 --> 01:02:22.383
outstanding in beauty
and at a blooming age.

01:02:23.490 --> 01:02:25.660
From Livy's perspective,

01:02:25.660 --> 01:02:29.310
Masinissa heard Sophonisba's speech

01:02:29.310 --> 01:02:32.520
more as blandishments of a lover

01:02:32.520 --> 01:02:35.250
rather than entreaties of a suppliant

01:02:36.730 --> 01:02:37.980
because he is a Numidian

01:02:37.980 --> 01:02:42.980
and the race of Numidians
is inclined towards passion.

01:02:43.900 --> 01:02:45.633
That is a quote from Livy.

01:02:46.980 --> 01:02:49.370
As a consequence, Livy says,

01:02:49.370 --> 01:02:54.370
quote, the victor is captured
by the love for the captive.

01:02:55.910 --> 01:02:58.267
The subsequent marriage between Sophonisba

01:02:58.267 --> 01:03:01.720
and Masinissa also gives Livy the context

01:03:01.720 --> 01:03:06.720
in which to contrast the
recklessness of Masinissa

01:03:06.750 --> 01:03:09.177
and the self control of Scipio.

01:03:10.550 --> 01:03:15.120
Livy focuses on Scipio showing
his controlled handling

01:03:15.120 --> 01:03:17.220
of Syphax's defection,

01:03:17.220 --> 01:03:19.330
as well as his disappointment

01:03:19.330 --> 01:03:20.920
and even anger

01:03:21.780 --> 01:03:26.780
over Massinissa's yielding
to his baser instincts.

01:03:28.030 --> 01:03:30.310
When responding to why he turned

01:03:30.310 --> 01:03:33.078
from his alliance with Rome,

01:03:33.078 --> 01:03:35.530
Syphax places the blame

01:03:35.530 --> 01:03:38.533
on his having wedded a Carthaginian woman.

01:03:39.780 --> 01:03:43.523
It was madness, bred from passion.

01:03:44.960 --> 01:03:47.780
More worrisome to Scipio,

01:03:47.780 --> 01:03:50.520
but consistent with Liny's view

01:03:50.520 --> 01:03:54.620
that lack of restraint has
unfortunate consequences,

01:03:54.620 --> 01:03:58.290
is Syphax's observation that Masinissa was

01:03:58.290 --> 01:04:02.920
neither more prudent
nor restrained than he.

01:04:02.920 --> 01:04:07.920
In fact, Masinissa is less
cautious because of his youth.

01:04:10.772 --> 01:04:15.340
Massinissa's hasty, impulsive
marriage to Sophonisba

01:04:17.150 --> 01:04:21.120
supports Syphax's image of Masinissa

01:04:21.120 --> 01:04:23.400
and provides the most telling evidence

01:04:23.400 --> 01:04:27.300
to support Scorpio's growing anxiety.

01:04:27.300 --> 01:04:31.820
Scipio sees clearly the
possibility that Masinissa,

01:04:31.820 --> 01:04:35.770
although he's been very
loyal up to this point,

01:04:35.770 --> 01:04:39.420
might now turn to the Carthaginian cause

01:04:39.420 --> 01:04:42.150
due to the influence of Sophonisba.

01:04:43.109 --> 01:04:46.120
In addition to his anxiety,

01:04:46.120 --> 01:04:48.203
Scipio is disgusted,

01:04:49.040 --> 01:04:54.030
disgusted because following mos maiorum,

01:04:54.030 --> 01:04:56.530
he practiced self-control

01:04:56.530 --> 01:05:00.840
and resisted the
beautiful female prisoners

01:05:00.840 --> 01:05:02.683
when he was in Spain.

01:05:04.100 --> 01:05:06.202
When Masinissa arrives,

01:05:06.202 --> 01:05:09.290
Scipio receives him warmly

01:05:09.290 --> 01:05:12.660
and lavishes public praise on him

01:05:12.660 --> 01:05:14.973
for his military accomplishments.

01:05:15.850 --> 01:05:17.780
There follows, however,

01:05:17.780 --> 01:05:20.000
a private

01:05:21.230 --> 01:05:22.780
tongue lashing.

01:05:22.780 --> 01:05:24.850
This is Scipio,

01:05:24.850 --> 01:05:28.540
but of these virtues
for which I would seem

01:05:28.540 --> 01:05:30.943
to have been sought out by you,

01:05:32.200 --> 01:05:37.200
I should've been prouder of
none as much as my self control

01:05:37.560 --> 01:05:40.893
and my resistance to bodily lust.

01:05:43.080 --> 01:05:46.460
How I wish that you had added this also

01:05:46.460 --> 01:05:49.983
to your other outstanding
virtues, Masinissa.

01:05:51.340 --> 01:05:56.200
There is not, absolutely not, believe me

01:05:56.200 --> 01:05:59.980
as much danger to men of our age

01:05:59.980 --> 01:06:04.980
from armed enemies as
from the constant presence

01:06:05.510 --> 01:06:08.603
of sensuality all around us.

01:06:09.720 --> 01:06:12.940
The man who reigns these in

01:06:12.940 --> 01:06:17.940
and tames them with his
own self-control has earned

01:06:18.310 --> 01:06:23.150
for himself more honor
and a greater victory

01:06:23.150 --> 01:06:27.093
than we have with the conquest of Syphax.

01:06:28.431 --> 01:06:29.264
Oh my goodness,

01:06:29.264 --> 01:06:32.570
he's a self-righteous
little something, ain't he?

01:06:32.570 --> 01:06:33.530
All right.

01:06:33.530 --> 01:06:37.000
Livy demonstrates that Masinissa,

01:06:37.000 --> 01:06:40.950
passionate Numidian though he may be,

01:06:40.950 --> 01:06:43.500
is really more Roman than the Romans

01:06:43.500 --> 01:06:46.703
and shares many of their ancient values.

01:06:48.150 --> 01:06:50.010
However, Masinissa,

01:06:50.010 --> 01:06:53.130
like Livy's contemporary Romans,

01:06:53.130 --> 01:06:55.760
has lost sight of these virtues.

01:06:55.760 --> 01:06:58.230
Scipio's exhortation to Masinissa,

01:06:58.230 --> 01:07:01.590
not to destroy the many good qualities

01:07:01.590 --> 01:07:05.410
with the one defective
passion could well be

01:07:05.410 --> 01:07:08.523
Livy's words to his fellow Romans.

01:07:10.120 --> 01:07:14.210
Masinissa fully grasped the
logic of Scipio's speech

01:07:14.210 --> 01:07:17.210
and like many colonized people,

01:07:17.210 --> 01:07:19.463
is caught between two worlds.

01:07:20.320 --> 01:07:22.620
Does he abandon the colonizer

01:07:22.620 --> 01:07:25.880
and lose all the political
advantages that might flow

01:07:25.880 --> 01:07:27.153
from that alliance?

01:07:28.560 --> 01:07:30.830
Or does he become a race traitor

01:07:30.830 --> 01:07:33.473
and turn over the woman he loves to Rome?

01:07:34.590 --> 01:07:37.050
After privately venting his grief,

01:07:37.050 --> 01:07:40.560
he hits a kind of compromise of sorts

01:07:40.560 --> 01:07:43.960
and provide Sophonisba
with the opportunity

01:07:43.960 --> 01:07:45.363
to commit suicide.

01:07:46.570 --> 01:07:49.210
When she accepts the cup of poison,

01:07:49.210 --> 01:07:53.240
she reverts back to her
previous gender performance

01:07:53.240 --> 01:07:55.023
of the obedient woman.

01:07:56.210 --> 01:08:00.410
By moving from a passive,
silent and obedient daughter

01:08:00.410 --> 01:08:04.120
to a forthright vocal
agent of her own sexual

01:08:04.120 --> 01:08:05.890
and political destiny,

01:08:05.890 --> 01:08:09.810
she violates the norm of Roman femininity

01:08:09.810 --> 01:08:13.920
and becomes a Cleopatra-esque exemplar

01:08:13.920 --> 01:08:16.453
of a corrupting foreign beauty.

01:08:17.600 --> 01:08:19.380
When she accepts the poison,

01:08:19.380 --> 01:08:22.260
she moves back to the obedient,

01:08:22.260 --> 01:08:27.170
although certainly not
passive or silent wife

01:08:27.170 --> 01:08:29.590
and restores order to the Roman male

01:08:29.590 --> 01:08:32.403
view of gender norms in the time of Livy.

01:08:33.260 --> 01:08:35.940
The speech that livy gives Sophonisba

01:08:35.940 --> 01:08:38.240
as she receives the poison

01:08:38.240 --> 01:08:41.640
places her in the tradition of Lucretia,

01:08:41.640 --> 01:08:45.893
the noble Roman woman who
commits suicide for honor.

01:08:47.600 --> 01:08:52.600
And I quote, I received this wedding gift,

01:08:52.810 --> 01:08:55.100
one not unwelcome,

01:08:55.100 --> 01:08:59.623
if a husband is able to offer
his wife nothing greater.

01:09:00.910 --> 01:09:05.910
But tell him this, I would
have died a happier death

01:09:06.810 --> 01:09:10.963
if I had not wed on the day of my funeral.

01:09:12.670 --> 01:09:16.230
For this she earns
praise from Livy, quote,

01:09:16.230 --> 01:09:21.230
she spoke calmly as she drained
fearlessly the received cup

01:09:22.430 --> 01:09:27.430
with absolutely no indication
of trepidation, end quote.

01:09:29.120 --> 01:09:31.360
Even though Scipio loses a trophy

01:09:31.360 --> 01:09:33.600
to be paraded in his triumph,

01:09:33.600 --> 01:09:35.003
who does that sound like?

01:09:35.860 --> 01:09:39.480
He gained so much more
because of Sophonisba.

01:09:39.480 --> 01:09:42.680
His crisis of masculinity is resolved

01:09:42.680 --> 01:09:44.950
by the assimilation of an African man

01:09:44.950 --> 01:09:49.490
who becomes a shining example
of Roman racialized gender

01:09:49.490 --> 01:09:52.360
and by the suicide of an African woman

01:09:52.360 --> 01:09:55.560
whose beauty might have dismantled

01:09:55.560 --> 01:09:57.523
the Roman imperial agenda.

01:09:58.890 --> 01:10:01.350
I hope I have shown that the intersection

01:10:01.350 --> 01:10:05.880
of race and gender succinctly
expressed in the concept

01:10:05.880 --> 01:10:09.570
of racialized gender is a useful tool

01:10:09.570 --> 01:10:12.924
in analyzing the ancient societies

01:10:12.924 --> 01:10:15.290
of Hellenic and Roman peoples.

01:10:15.290 --> 01:10:19.440
Donna Haraway makes the argument that,

01:10:19.440 --> 01:10:23.920
quote, neither gender nor race
is something with an origin

01:10:23.920 --> 01:10:27.200
or example in the family

01:10:27.200 --> 01:10:31.620
that then travels out into
the rest of the social world.

01:10:31.620 --> 01:10:36.620
Rather, gender and race
are built into practice,

01:10:38.020 --> 01:10:38.893
end quote.

01:10:39.730 --> 01:10:44.310
This is as true in
antiquity as it is today.

01:10:44.310 --> 01:10:45.653
Thank you very much.

01:10:48.560 --> 01:10:50.010
- Thank you so much, Shelley,

01:10:51.870 --> 01:10:53.210
for that wonderful detailed talk

01:10:53.210 --> 01:10:54.903
and all those great quotations.

01:10:56.970 --> 01:10:58.260
We will take questions,

01:10:58.260 --> 01:10:59.840
Professor Haley will take questions now.

01:10:59.840 --> 01:11:02.930
I would invite everyone
to either put their name

01:11:02.930 --> 01:11:04.900
in the chat so I can see you

01:11:04.900 --> 01:11:08.250
and call on you since there
are many people on this call

01:11:08.250 --> 01:11:11.470
or to simply put their
question directly into the chat

01:11:11.470 --> 01:11:14.016
and I will relay, I will read

01:11:14.016 --> 01:11:14.849
and relay questions to Professor Haley

01:11:14.849 --> 01:11:16.523
just to make this smoother.

01:11:18.130 --> 01:11:21.420
So, Shelley, there are
already a few questions

01:11:21.420 --> 01:11:26.190
and I'm gonna just go
through them one by one.

01:11:26.190 --> 01:11:30.310
So the first question
you have is about the,

01:11:30.310 --> 01:11:32.150
what happens to the biracial children

01:11:32.150 --> 01:11:34.144
of Medea and Cleopatra?

01:11:34.144 --> 01:11:36.560
And the question Lora
Holland Goldthwaite notes

01:11:37.490 --> 01:11:40.100
that both sets of children are killed

01:11:40.100 --> 01:11:42.740
and she asks, have you
noted instances of this kind

01:11:42.740 --> 01:11:45.970
of hybridization being
considered monstrous somehow

01:11:45.970 --> 01:11:49.123
and therefore such
offspring simply had to die?

01:11:52.290 --> 01:11:53.723
- Hmm, okay.

01:11:57.570 --> 01:12:02.570
To the second question
about monstrosity, right?

01:12:05.380 --> 01:12:06.703
Yes and no.

01:12:11.630 --> 01:12:12.913
Who was also biracial.

01:12:14.530 --> 01:12:17.210
He had to die, right?

01:12:17.210 --> 01:12:19.630
But that was a political calculation,

01:12:19.630 --> 01:12:24.623
that wasn't based on the fact
that he was biracial, right?

01:12:28.009 --> 01:12:29.410
The children of,

01:12:29.410 --> 01:12:31.033
the twins, for example,

01:12:32.120 --> 01:12:35.113
Alexander Helios and Cleopatra Selene,

01:12:36.210 --> 01:12:38.250
seem to have had very happy lives

01:12:39.360 --> 01:12:43.583
after the death of their parents.

01:12:46.070 --> 01:12:49.660
It always amazes me that
that's how it's described by

01:12:52.160 --> 01:12:52.993
folks,

01:12:53.950 --> 01:12:57.893
after all, they were
raised by Octavia, right?

01:12:58.782 --> 01:13:00.423
She reared them.

01:13:02.413 --> 01:13:04.250
We know that Cleopatra Selene went on

01:13:04.250 --> 01:13:06.850
to become a ruler in her own right

01:13:06.850 --> 01:13:08.443
with Juba II.

01:13:12.362 --> 01:13:13.195
And so,

01:13:14.870 --> 01:13:15.810
from all accounts,

01:13:15.810 --> 01:13:17.973
Alexander was,

01:13:20.320 --> 01:13:22.630
as far as we can tell,

01:13:22.630 --> 01:13:25.270
certainly he adjusted better

01:13:25.270 --> 01:13:28.690
than let's say Caligula or Nero.

01:13:28.690 --> 01:13:31.103
So he,

01:13:33.076 --> 01:13:35.993
he seemed to have, you know, a fine life.

01:13:36.930 --> 01:13:39.720
So I don't believe,

01:13:39.720 --> 01:13:43.763
I mean, I've never read
any negative descriptions

01:13:45.130 --> 01:13:48.790
of biracial children

01:13:50.530 --> 01:13:52.693
in the sources.

01:13:53.869 --> 01:13:54.702
So,

01:13:56.490 --> 01:13:57.323
yeah,

01:13:58.260 --> 01:14:01.530
and I think it's because we're,

01:14:01.530 --> 01:14:06.530
we're sort of dealing with the
same class of people, right,

01:14:06.690 --> 01:14:11.690
where it's not like they're
different class statuses here,

01:14:13.140 --> 01:14:14.423
so,

01:14:17.180 --> 01:14:18.013
yeah.

01:14:20.160 --> 01:14:23.493
- Thanks, Suzanne Li has a question.

01:14:23.493 --> 01:14:25.203
Suzanne, would you like
to unmute yourself?

01:14:26.730 --> 01:14:28.203
- Hi, can you hear me?

01:14:29.044 --> 01:14:30.060
- [Shelley and Ayesha] Yes.

01:14:30.060 --> 01:14:30.900
- Fantastic.

01:14:30.900 --> 01:14:32.740
Thank you so much.

01:14:32.740 --> 01:14:33.573
It's great to be here with everybody

01:14:33.573 --> 01:14:34.450
and with you, Shelley.

01:14:36.054 --> 01:14:37.610
And I know we've talked about
this a little bit before

01:14:37.610 --> 01:14:38.700
'cause, you know,

01:14:38.700 --> 01:14:39.533
I've always,

01:14:40.822 --> 01:14:41.780
and so in the context of,

01:14:41.780 --> 01:14:43.300
I've always thought of
like race and gender is

01:14:43.300 --> 01:14:45.690
like sliding scales that intersect,

01:14:45.690 --> 01:14:47.240
we've talked about that before.

01:14:48.440 --> 01:14:50.710
And my question for you,

01:14:50.710 --> 01:14:52.620
like as I was listening to your talk,

01:14:52.620 --> 01:14:53.630
was that, you know,

01:14:53.630 --> 01:14:55.130
it seems like foreignness,

01:14:55.130 --> 01:14:56.610
or using that word foreignness,

01:14:56.610 --> 01:14:58.890
which seems to keep coming up,

01:14:58.890 --> 01:15:02.513
already implies female gender in it.

01:15:03.790 --> 01:15:07.240
So even when it's used to
describe a man it's, you know,

01:15:07.240 --> 01:15:08.460
whether man or woman, right,

01:15:08.460 --> 01:15:10.750
it implies a single gender,

01:15:10.750 --> 01:15:13.550
but it doesn't seem to
work in the other way.

01:15:13.550 --> 01:15:15.170
Like, so describing a woman,

01:15:15.170 --> 01:15:17.270
that doesn't necessarily seem to,

01:15:17.270 --> 01:15:18.103
or does it?

01:15:18.103 --> 01:15:19.370
That's the question, I guess.

01:15:19.370 --> 01:15:20.680
It doesn't seem to, like,

01:15:20.680 --> 01:15:23.520
it seems like one is more
distinctly pointing, you know,

01:15:23.520 --> 01:15:25.520
towards the two,

01:15:25.520 --> 01:15:27.280
whereas if you say,

01:15:27.280 --> 01:15:28.260
like if you look at a woman,

01:15:28.260 --> 01:15:30.185
a Greek woman or a Roman woman

01:15:30.185 --> 01:15:31.983
and just describe her as a woman,

01:15:31.983 --> 01:15:34.143
does that imply for foreignness to it?

01:15:37.222 --> 01:15:38.312
- Hmm,

01:15:38.312 --> 01:15:40.420
that's a really, really great question

01:15:40.420 --> 01:15:45.420
and the first thing that
springs in my mind is,

01:15:45.680 --> 01:15:48.840
and I think it kind of
supports your point that

01:15:51.880 --> 01:15:56.280
feminacy is implied in foreignness, right?

01:15:56.280 --> 01:15:57.940
And that's when,

01:15:57.940 --> 01:16:02.890
when Iarbas is praying to his father,

01:16:02.890 --> 01:16:04.456
Jupiter Hammon,

01:16:04.456 --> 01:16:06.370
to

01:16:06.370 --> 01:16:09.340
put an end to this affair

01:16:10.300 --> 01:16:12.703
between Dido and Aeneas,

01:16:14.160 --> 01:16:17.560
the way that he describes Aeneas, right,

01:16:17.560 --> 01:16:19.950
the description that he gives,

01:16:19.950 --> 01:16:22.757
he calls him a semi weir, right?

01:16:22.757 --> 01:16:25.200
He calls him the semi weir

01:16:25.200 --> 01:16:29.660
and his hair is dripping
with perfumed oils.

01:16:29.660 --> 01:16:31.100
Excuse me.

01:16:31.100 --> 01:16:32.960
So everything is pointing to the fact

01:16:32.960 --> 01:16:35.323
that he is not a man, right?

01:16:36.295 --> 01:16:40.370
And the same with that invective

01:16:40.370 --> 01:16:42.400
that Cassius Dio makes up

01:16:43.892 --> 01:16:45.860
about Octavion, right?

01:16:45.860 --> 01:16:47.363
He says, you know,

01:16:48.300 --> 01:16:50.720
anyone who is

01:16:50.720 --> 01:16:53.430
treated or lives the life like

01:16:54.544 --> 01:16:57.940
of luxury like a woman
shouldn't be called a man.

01:16:57.940 --> 01:16:59.240
So yes,

01:16:59.240 --> 01:17:02.863
I think it's true that it's another,

01:17:05.500 --> 01:17:09.300
it's another way to be misogynistic,

01:17:09.300 --> 01:17:10.640
right?

01:17:10.640 --> 01:17:12.963
But I still think that,

01:17:15.260 --> 01:17:16.093
yeah,

01:17:17.240 --> 01:17:19.070
it definitely,

01:17:19.070 --> 01:17:20.973
the women get the,

01:17:21.950 --> 01:17:25.260
the shorter end of things.

01:17:25.260 --> 01:17:26.450
You rarely

01:17:27.540 --> 01:17:30.490
see women.

01:17:30.490 --> 01:17:33.170
Well, except in myth, right,

01:17:33.170 --> 01:17:36.720
when women are masculinized,

01:17:36.720 --> 01:17:40.445
then they sort of become monsters, right?

01:17:40.445 --> 01:17:41.278
- Yeah, I was gonna ask.

01:17:41.278 --> 01:17:43.920
There seems to be a monster
scale somewhere on here as well-

01:17:43.920 --> 01:17:45.628
- Yeah, yeah, that's right.

01:17:45.628 --> 01:17:46.690
That's right.

01:17:46.690 --> 01:17:48.030
That's right.

01:17:48.030 --> 01:17:49.560
- Dr. Murray has talked
a bit about it too.

01:17:49.560 --> 01:17:51.530
- Right, right.

01:17:51.530 --> 01:17:53.253
So, yeah.

01:17:55.960 --> 01:17:58.500
I mean, racialized gender,

01:17:58.500 --> 01:17:59.573
at least,

01:18:00.730 --> 01:18:02.885
modern way.

01:18:02.885 --> 01:18:05.640
I mean, I always try to hope

01:18:05.640 --> 01:18:08.630
that things are a bit
better in the ancient world,

01:18:08.630 --> 01:18:10.183
but that's not always the case.

01:18:11.330 --> 01:18:15.580
But certainly racialized gender is meant

01:18:15.580 --> 01:18:17.720
to describe a kind of oppression

01:18:17.720 --> 01:18:18.640
and so

01:18:21.689 --> 01:18:23.439
it is true that women

01:18:25.851 --> 01:18:26.684
are,

01:18:27.617 --> 01:18:30.530
it's not a positive, right?

01:18:30.530 --> 01:18:33.163
The outcome of it is never positive.

01:18:34.958 --> 01:18:38.277
And I think that's probably
also true in antiquity,

01:18:39.970 --> 01:18:42.900
especially if the woman is also foreign.

01:18:42.900 --> 01:18:45.053
I mean, it would be
interesting to look at,

01:18:46.360 --> 01:18:49.940
well, let's say Fulvia, right?

01:18:49.940 --> 01:18:51.883
Fulvia violates every,

01:18:53.020 --> 01:18:54.210
whatever,

01:18:54.210 --> 01:18:58.090
bullet point you want in
terms of Roman femininity.

01:18:58.090 --> 01:19:01.010
Fulvia violates those, right?

01:19:01.010 --> 01:19:03.810
And so she gets described,

01:19:03.810 --> 01:19:04.780
you know,

01:19:04.780 --> 01:19:07.173
I mean she is masculinized, right,

01:19:08.370 --> 01:19:09.900
based on her actions,

01:19:09.900 --> 01:19:12.963
the fact that she leads
an army and she, right?

01:19:15.168 --> 01:19:16.360
And what is it Plutarch says,

01:19:16.360 --> 01:19:17.460
at one point he says,

01:19:17.460 --> 01:19:20.980
Cleopatra should thank Fulvia for

01:19:23.120 --> 01:19:24.200
training

01:19:25.050 --> 01:19:26.820
Anthony

01:19:26.820 --> 01:19:29.510
to yield to the control of a woman

01:19:29.510 --> 01:19:30.950
or something.

01:19:30.950 --> 01:19:33.570
So it's, yeah.

01:19:33.570 --> 01:19:34.660
Yeah.

01:19:34.660 --> 01:19:36.810
But that's a really,

01:19:36.810 --> 01:19:37.990
I'm gonna have to think about that,

01:19:37.990 --> 01:19:40.860
that point, that it's a
really interesting one

01:19:40.860 --> 01:19:42.433
that it's still,

01:19:45.010 --> 01:19:49.500
womanhood isn't raised up
because of racialized gender,

01:19:49.500 --> 01:19:51.940
it's still considered

01:19:54.139 --> 01:19:56.127
kind of the lower end of things, right?

01:19:57.141 --> 01:19:58.171
Yeah.

01:19:58.171 --> 01:19:59.012
Yeah.

01:19:59.012 --> 01:19:59.845
- Thank you, Shelley.

01:19:59.845 --> 01:20:01.360
So here's another question from the chat

01:20:01.360 --> 01:20:02.540
and I think this is a question

01:20:02.540 --> 01:20:04.920
that you've certainly thought more about

01:20:04.920 --> 01:20:07.378
and alluded to early on in your talk.

01:20:07.378 --> 01:20:08.920
But the questioner asks you

01:20:08.920 --> 01:20:11.100
to reflect further on the
difference between ethnicity

01:20:11.100 --> 01:20:14.430
and race and suggests
that Greek was perhaps

01:20:14.430 --> 01:20:15.460
a linguistic category

01:20:15.460 --> 01:20:17.960
and he writes a lot of
literature written in Greek

01:20:17.960 --> 01:20:19.530
after Alexander was written by people

01:20:19.530 --> 01:20:22.172
who were not born or raised Greek

01:20:22.172 --> 01:20:23.390
and that barbarians, opposite of Greeks,

01:20:23.390 --> 01:20:24.760
were people who did not speak Greek

01:20:24.760 --> 01:20:26.980
and so I wonder if you can reflect

01:20:26.980 --> 01:20:28.690
on the question of kind of language,

01:20:28.690 --> 01:20:29.650
ethnicity and race

01:20:29.650 --> 01:20:32.920
and how you use the word
race and racialization.

01:20:32.920 --> 01:20:33.753
- Right.

01:20:35.310 --> 01:20:36.253
So,

01:20:37.600 --> 01:20:39.053
I just find,

01:20:39.940 --> 01:20:42.600
particularly for those of us

01:20:43.950 --> 01:20:44.783
who

01:20:46.318 --> 01:20:47.410
want to be

01:20:48.980 --> 01:20:49.870
careful

01:20:49.870 --> 01:20:51.393
and want to,

01:20:52.350 --> 01:20:54.260
I don't wanna to make a mistake

01:20:54.260 --> 01:20:56.740
or don't wanna say the wrong thing

01:20:56.740 --> 01:20:57.573
or

01:20:59.780 --> 01:21:03.250
that I think sometimes, not always,

01:21:03.250 --> 01:21:07.030
but sometimes ethnicity
becomes a code word

01:21:08.340 --> 01:21:12.930
or a euphemism when race would

01:21:12.930 --> 01:21:14.663
work better, right?

01:21:15.720 --> 01:21:18.754
I'm thinking particularly,

01:21:18.754 --> 01:21:20.230
I note that

01:21:21.220 --> 01:21:26.220
a lot of my colleagues in a broad sense,

01:21:27.830 --> 01:21:30.930
fear that if they talk about race

01:21:30.930 --> 01:21:33.803
then that makes them racist.

01:21:34.950 --> 01:21:38.520
And the two things are
very different, right?

01:21:38.520 --> 01:21:40.143
You can talk about race,

01:21:44.730 --> 01:21:47.720
that someone is African American,

01:21:47.720 --> 01:21:50.113
that doesn't make you racist, right?

01:21:52.241 --> 01:21:54.580
So I absolutely agree.

01:21:54.580 --> 01:21:58.001
I mean, let's just take Latinx.

01:21:58.001 --> 01:22:02.310
Latinx is a linguistic category,

01:22:02.310 --> 01:22:03.143
right?

01:22:04.947 --> 01:22:05.850
So

01:22:05.850 --> 01:22:10.530
folks who are Latinx can be white

01:22:10.530 --> 01:22:13.840
or they can be of African descent, right,

01:22:13.840 --> 01:22:16.420
or that can be Asian, right?

01:22:16.420 --> 01:22:21.420
So yes, there definitely
are linguistic categories.

01:22:24.003 --> 01:22:25.880
I have the problem,

01:22:25.880 --> 01:22:28.303
the problem I have with,

01:22:30.370 --> 01:22:34.160
with Greek is that, Greek, you know,

01:22:34.160 --> 01:22:36.590
when we're talking
about the Greeks, right?

01:22:36.590 --> 01:22:38.440
It's a little like

01:22:39.620 --> 01:22:42.070
the modern day umbrella,

01:22:42.070 --> 01:22:44.920
and folks here can correct
me if I get this wrong,

01:22:44.920 --> 01:22:48.459
but the modern day umbrella

01:22:48.459 --> 01:22:50.750
of Asian,

01:22:50.750 --> 01:22:51.583
right?

01:22:51.583 --> 01:22:52.970
Oh my goodness.

01:22:52.970 --> 01:22:57.240
I mean, that's a huge
category of people, right?

01:22:57.240 --> 01:22:59.853
And so you,

01:23:03.060 --> 01:23:04.587
you lose the,

01:23:06.205 --> 01:23:10.730
the specificity when you
always talk about the Greeks,

01:23:10.730 --> 01:23:13.483
but who are you really talking about?

01:23:14.700 --> 01:23:16.250
I would say 95% of the time,

01:23:16.250 --> 01:23:19.233
you're actually talking
about the Athenians, right?

01:23:21.400 --> 01:23:24.850
And so I mean the Spartans
are different, right?

01:23:24.850 --> 01:23:28.090
That's, I mean, that's the
most obvious group that,

01:23:28.090 --> 01:23:30.900
who is different to the Athenians,

01:23:30.900 --> 01:23:34.350
but there were the Lesbians,

01:23:34.350 --> 01:23:35.850
there were the Corinthians,

01:23:35.850 --> 01:23:37.500
there were the,

01:23:37.500 --> 01:23:40.210
all these get lumped in under Greek

01:23:40.210 --> 01:23:44.763
and I don't wanna lose that specificity.

01:23:45.750 --> 01:23:48.370
I also want us to

01:23:51.800 --> 01:23:53.570
really think about

01:23:57.610 --> 01:23:58.970
race,

01:23:58.970 --> 01:24:02.730
less as something that's physical

01:24:02.730 --> 01:24:06.543
and more something that's cultural, right?

01:24:07.920 --> 01:24:11.500
And that too is problematic, okay?

01:24:11.500 --> 01:24:15.160
Because that opens it up to
all sorts of things like,

01:24:15.160 --> 01:24:18.827
oh, so that means Rachel Dolezal is okay

01:24:20.648 --> 01:24:23.870
and I'm going, no, no,
no, no, no, no she's not,

01:24:23.870 --> 01:24:27.130
no, that's not okay, no, right?

01:24:27.130 --> 01:24:29.480
But just to kind of,

01:24:29.480 --> 01:24:31.268
what we have to do is we have

01:24:31.268 --> 01:24:34.120
to break the stranglehold of,

01:24:34.120 --> 01:24:36.633
of the white supremacist,

01:24:37.600 --> 01:24:38.770
bio

01:24:40.280 --> 01:24:44.470
racism that

01:24:44.470 --> 01:24:46.440
developed, right?

01:24:46.440 --> 01:24:48.672
That's what I'm trying to do,

01:24:48.672 --> 01:24:51.030
trying to break us out of that

01:24:51.030 --> 01:24:53.600
and think more broadly

01:24:53.600 --> 01:24:57.130
about what race could have been, right,

01:24:57.130 --> 01:24:59.270
in antiquity, right,

01:24:59.270 --> 01:25:00.640
And it's hard,

01:25:00.640 --> 01:25:04.273
I'm not suggesting that
all of this is easy, right?

01:25:06.952 --> 01:25:07.785
And yeah, so-

01:25:08.940 --> 01:25:10.720
- Shelley, I'm gonna
exercise my prerogative here

01:25:10.720 --> 01:25:11.553
as the moderator,

01:25:11.553 --> 01:25:13.130
just sort of wonder with you

01:25:13.130 --> 01:25:15.240
as a non-classicist in my own field

01:25:15.240 --> 01:25:17.560
which is early modern studies.

01:25:17.560 --> 01:25:19.090
The early modern studies
and medieval studies have

01:25:19.090 --> 01:25:20.140
of course have been roiled

01:25:20.140 --> 01:25:22.620
by similar kinds of
conversations about, you know,

01:25:22.620 --> 01:25:23.597
how do we talk about race?

01:25:23.597 --> 01:25:26.530
And one, one pretty
strong answer coming out

01:25:26.530 --> 01:25:29.840
of critical race theory has
been to sort of think about race

01:25:29.840 --> 01:25:31.410
as a social category,

01:25:31.410 --> 01:25:34.390
as about realities of hierarchization,

01:25:34.390 --> 01:25:38.150
about forms of power and
how they are exercised

01:25:38.150 --> 01:25:40.690
and to understand them less
as being inscribed on the body

01:25:40.690 --> 01:25:42.800
in a biological sense, which is of course,

01:25:42.800 --> 01:25:44.930
one way in which race is conceived

01:25:44.930 --> 01:25:46.600
in the 19th century, primarily,

01:25:46.600 --> 01:25:50.260
but to explore the mechanisms
of social organization

01:25:50.260 --> 01:25:52.850
and race as the term that names that.

01:25:52.850 --> 01:25:54.670
So for instance, in the
early modern period,

01:25:54.670 --> 01:25:57.327
historians have worked on
the term, natial, all right,

01:25:57.327 --> 01:25:58.623
and the ideal nation.

01:25:59.570 --> 01:26:03.000
How is that in fact a
racialized category perhaps,

01:26:03.000 --> 01:26:04.440
not perhaps race in the sense

01:26:04.440 --> 01:26:07.440
we might understand it
post 19th and 20th century,

01:26:07.440 --> 01:26:09.680
but in a term that
carries that same charge

01:26:09.680 --> 01:26:11.080
of social organization

01:26:11.080 --> 01:26:12.550
and I wonder if that is helpful

01:26:12.550 --> 01:26:14.623
in the classical context as well?

01:26:19.120 --> 01:26:22.303
- Yes, I mean I,

01:26:23.150 --> 01:26:25.893
just off the top of my
head, I would say, yes.

01:26:27.520 --> 01:26:31.923
I think one thing that often gets,

01:26:33.060 --> 01:26:35.750
it kind of gets alighted,

01:26:35.750 --> 01:26:39.933
is the sense of class.

01:26:41.235 --> 01:26:43.210
It's very easy,

01:26:43.210 --> 01:26:46.010
I mean all the people, right,

01:26:46.010 --> 01:26:48.800
even Medea, for that matter,

01:26:48.800 --> 01:26:50.183
even though she's mythical,

01:26:51.370 --> 01:26:54.370
ringing in my head is
what my children would say

01:26:54.370 --> 01:26:56.220
to me when they were little

01:26:56.220 --> 01:26:59.140
and I was sort of struggling

01:26:59.140 --> 01:27:00.803
with these things that was going on.

01:27:00.803 --> 01:27:03.150
I think it was Dido I
was going on and on about

01:27:03.150 --> 01:27:06.177
and both my son and
daughter would say to me,

01:27:06.177 --> 01:27:09.450
"Mom, mom, calm down.

01:27:09.450 --> 01:27:12.867
They're fictional." (laughing)

01:27:14.130 --> 01:27:16.993
So I don't wanna invest
too much in Medea but,

01:27:20.150 --> 01:27:21.850
now I forgot what I was gonna say.

01:27:25.580 --> 01:27:27.630
Everyone I talked about, right,

01:27:27.630 --> 01:27:29.710
is of the ruling class.

01:27:29.710 --> 01:27:31.940
Every single one, every single one.

01:27:31.940 --> 01:27:36.830
And so when you look at
things like graffiti, right,

01:27:36.830 --> 01:27:38.700
the graffiti in Pompeii.

01:27:38.700 --> 01:27:41.356
Woohoo, that, hmm, yeah,

01:27:41.356 --> 01:27:45.660
what does it mean when someone describes,

01:27:45.660 --> 01:27:49.480
you know, that they're
in love with someone and

01:27:51.560 --> 01:27:53.120
she happens to be

01:27:54.810 --> 01:27:56.527
a black woman.

01:27:56.527 --> 01:27:58.640
That's how she described, negra, right?

01:27:58.640 --> 01:28:00.520
She's described as a black woman

01:28:00.520 --> 01:28:03.580
but the candida women,

01:28:03.580 --> 01:28:08.260
which we're not gonna translate as white,

01:28:08.260 --> 01:28:12.200
the candida woman is jealous
and gets angry about this.

01:28:12.200 --> 01:28:14.870
Well, you know,

01:28:14.870 --> 01:28:16.880
we're not talking about
the ruling class now,

01:28:16.880 --> 01:28:20.203
where we're talking about
just sort of everyday folks.

01:28:21.120 --> 01:28:22.363
So,

01:28:24.090 --> 01:28:24.923
I think,

01:28:25.906 --> 01:28:28.700
then I think the social probably works.

01:28:28.700 --> 01:28:31.250
I think if you get out of the

01:28:33.085 --> 01:28:33.918
political class

01:28:35.010 --> 01:28:37.310
and you're looking at sort
of everyday kind of folks,

01:28:37.310 --> 01:28:38.143
I think

01:28:40.410 --> 01:28:44.430
definitely raises a social category

01:28:44.430 --> 01:28:45.570
rather than

01:28:49.401 --> 01:28:51.843
the status that we have certainly works.

01:28:52.830 --> 01:28:54.070
I would say, yeah.

01:28:54.070 --> 01:28:55.370
- Shelley, one more question.

01:28:55.370 --> 01:28:56.850
Jeff, you should be-

01:28:56.850 --> 01:28:58.359
- Oh, thank you.

01:28:58.359 --> 01:28:59.192
Thank you very much.

01:28:59.192 --> 01:29:00.025
Thank you, Professor Haley.

01:29:00.025 --> 01:29:01.540
That was a fascinating talk

01:29:01.540 --> 01:29:02.850
and it was so wide ranging.

01:29:02.850 --> 01:29:04.728
I really enjoyed it

01:29:04.728 --> 01:29:05.561
and I hope I have, let's say,

01:29:05.561 --> 01:29:07.510
a relevant question.

01:29:07.510 --> 01:29:10.340
Thanks to our wonderful post-COVID world,

01:29:10.340 --> 01:29:12.450
a lot of us have been taking advantage

01:29:12.450 --> 01:29:14.380
of seeing a number of
talks that we might not

01:29:14.380 --> 01:29:17.320
otherwise get a chance
to see, including yours.

01:29:17.320 --> 01:29:19.030
I heard one last week
which was very interesting

01:29:19.030 --> 01:29:20.860
which is the construction of race

01:29:20.860 --> 01:29:22.950
in the early Lombard world

01:29:22.950 --> 01:29:24.550
and one of the observations,

01:29:24.550 --> 01:29:27.070
ethnicity's a better word maybe,

01:29:27.070 --> 01:29:29.190
one of her observations,

01:29:29.190 --> 01:29:31.850
also similarly focused on the association

01:29:31.850 --> 01:29:34.880
of male gender with regards to themselves.

01:29:34.880 --> 01:29:37.920
But I was kind of curious
about the rhetorical exercise

01:29:37.920 --> 01:29:39.650
of using barbarians,

01:29:39.650 --> 01:29:40.483
and I use that,

01:29:40.483 --> 01:29:41.930
put that in quotation marks,

01:29:41.930 --> 01:29:45.220
to point out the feminine, read weak,

01:29:45.220 --> 01:29:47.467
racial stereotypes in themselves

01:29:47.467 --> 01:29:48.637
and I was thinking of things

01:29:48.637 --> 01:29:51.600
like Tacitus' constructions
of Germanic identity

01:29:51.600 --> 01:29:55.150
or Procopius' constructions
of Ostrogothic masculinity

01:29:55.150 --> 01:29:57.400
relative to Romans, et cetera.

01:29:57.400 --> 01:29:59.380
And I guess my question
really is the degree

01:29:59.380 --> 01:30:00.950
to which civilization is perceived

01:30:00.950 --> 01:30:02.853
as feminine in these discourses.

01:30:05.060 --> 01:30:06.040
- Of course this,

01:30:06.040 --> 01:30:07.133
well, let's see.

01:30:08.020 --> 01:30:11.460
One way that folks,

01:30:11.460 --> 01:30:12.630
this is not me,

01:30:12.630 --> 01:30:16.210
this goes beyond me,

01:30:16.210 --> 01:30:18.870
but just in terms of

01:30:20.560 --> 01:30:22.270
group identity, right?

01:30:22.270 --> 01:30:24.810
So group identity formation,

01:30:24.810 --> 01:30:29.660
how do you determine what
your group identity is?

01:30:29.660 --> 01:30:32.220
And I do think this is trans-historical.

01:30:32.220 --> 01:30:34.673
I think this goes across ages.

01:30:37.416 --> 01:30:39.210
How do you determine

01:30:40.670 --> 01:30:42.293
who your group is, right?

01:30:43.774 --> 01:30:44.607
Right?

01:30:46.160 --> 01:30:48.260
There's a couple of ways to do that.

01:30:48.260 --> 01:30:51.389
You could do the both and, right?

01:30:51.389 --> 01:30:55.913
You could say, oh, my
people are other people

01:30:55.913 --> 01:30:57.950
who wear headphones

01:30:59.010 --> 01:31:00.940
and who are on Zoom.

01:31:00.940 --> 01:31:02.613
Those are my people, right?

01:31:05.000 --> 01:31:08.770
So it's a series of ands,
it's additive, right?

01:31:08.770 --> 01:31:13.340
Or you can do the exclusive, right?

01:31:13.340 --> 01:31:16.770
I determine who I am

01:31:16.770 --> 01:31:20.403
by checking off what I'm not,

01:31:21.850 --> 01:31:22.860
right?

01:31:22.860 --> 01:31:25.740
And so I don't know if that's
what you're talking about

01:31:25.740 --> 01:31:28.113
but I think you look,

01:31:29.066 --> 01:31:29.903
you certainly,

01:31:34.580 --> 01:31:35.857
establish the,

01:31:37.870 --> 01:31:39.320
I'm gonna to break my own rule,

01:31:39.320 --> 01:31:42.240
but the ethnic identity, right,

01:31:42.240 --> 01:31:45.100
of the Greeks, right?

01:31:45.100 --> 01:31:47.173
Who does he pit them against?

01:31:48.430 --> 01:31:49.263
Right?

01:31:49.263 --> 01:31:52.480
Does he say it's all the people who

01:31:53.710 --> 01:31:57.800
eat olives at lunchtime?

01:31:57.800 --> 01:31:58.670
No.

01:31:58.670 --> 01:32:00.040
He pits them

01:32:01.040 --> 01:32:02.950
against the enemy, right,

01:32:02.950 --> 01:32:04.470
who are the Persians,

01:32:04.470 --> 01:32:07.960
who do things very different to them.

01:32:07.960 --> 01:32:10.660
The same with his
description of the Egyptians.

01:32:10.660 --> 01:32:12.400
Oh my God!

01:32:12.400 --> 01:32:15.270
The Egyptians like

01:32:15.270 --> 01:32:16.933
have sex outdoors, what!

01:32:18.210 --> 01:32:19.180
What!

01:32:19.180 --> 01:32:22.450
They're barbarians, right?

01:32:22.450 --> 01:32:24.500
Or they pee outdoor, whatever,

01:32:24.500 --> 01:32:26.810
whatever he says the Egyptians do, right?

01:32:26.810 --> 01:32:27.643
So,

01:32:28.980 --> 01:32:29.813
yeah, it's

01:32:31.478 --> 01:32:34.080
if in the

01:32:34.080 --> 01:32:35.160
discourse

01:32:35.160 --> 01:32:37.530
of trying to

01:32:37.530 --> 01:32:39.640
do, right,

01:32:39.640 --> 01:32:41.820
whether you wanna call it racial formation

01:32:41.820 --> 01:32:46.120
or group identity formation.

01:32:46.120 --> 01:32:49.413
If it's a question of insider, outsider,

01:32:51.050 --> 01:32:52.980
then that, you know,

01:32:52.980 --> 01:32:54.020
and I think that's the way

01:32:54.020 --> 01:32:57.113
most Western societies
have done it, right?

01:32:58.700 --> 01:33:00.453
And maybe not just Western.

01:33:01.420 --> 01:33:04.910
Certainly any society that

01:33:07.230 --> 01:33:08.113
has,

01:33:09.020 --> 01:33:11.283
I think about in my own family,

01:33:13.960 --> 01:33:16.710
my great grandmother was a member

01:33:16.710 --> 01:33:18.870
of the Onondaga Nation, right,

01:33:18.870 --> 01:33:22.160
which is part of the Haudenosaunee, right?

01:33:22.160 --> 01:33:25.070
So, but when,

01:33:25.070 --> 01:33:29.960
when she married outside of the nation,

01:33:29.960 --> 01:33:32.166
as she did, right,

01:33:32.166 --> 01:33:32.999
and it didn't matter who it was,

01:33:32.999 --> 01:33:34.760
it happened to be an African American,

01:33:34.760 --> 01:33:37.630
but it didn't matter who it was,

01:33:37.630 --> 01:33:40.560
she was no longer a member, right,

01:33:40.560 --> 01:33:42.323
of that group.

01:33:43.280 --> 01:33:47.530
So that's the way they formed their,

01:33:47.530 --> 01:33:48.520
and still do,

01:33:48.520 --> 01:33:51.290
their identification.

01:33:51.290 --> 01:33:52.123
So,

01:33:54.600 --> 01:33:57.070
yeah, I don't know if that
answers your question, Jeff,

01:33:57.070 --> 01:33:57.903
but

01:33:58.840 --> 01:34:00.020
I think

01:34:02.520 --> 01:34:04.680
the outsider, the unknown,

01:34:04.680 --> 01:34:08.570
the foreigner is always gonna be portrayed

01:34:08.570 --> 01:34:10.560
as the barbarian.

01:34:10.560 --> 01:34:15.410
Worth is ethnocentrism
in a nutshell, right?

01:34:15.410 --> 01:34:17.803
So we're always superior.

01:34:18.730 --> 01:34:20.710
Heavens, we are, right?

01:34:20.710 --> 01:34:21.740
We're the best.

01:34:21.740 --> 01:34:24.963
American exceptionalism,
we're the best, right?

01:34:26.268 --> 01:34:27.197
And that's okay

01:34:29.900 --> 01:34:32.250
as long as there isn't value attached,

01:34:32.250 --> 01:34:36.400
as long as you don't go out and
shoot people because, right,

01:34:36.400 --> 01:34:38.843
they're not American, right?

01:34:40.100 --> 01:34:44.453
Americans are only people who
eat apple pie and whatever.

01:34:46.740 --> 01:34:49.040
I'm veering off into the silly now.

01:34:49.040 --> 01:34:53.180
But, yeah, so I hope that helped.

01:34:53.180 --> 01:34:54.520
- One final question.

01:34:54.520 --> 01:34:56.490
Tristan, I'm gonna invite you

01:34:56.490 --> 01:34:58.143
to unmute yourself in one second.

01:35:01.740 --> 01:35:03.620
- It's my friend, Tristan.

01:35:03.620 --> 01:35:04.815
- [Ayesha] Go ahead.

01:35:04.815 --> 01:35:07.100
- Hi, Tristan, how are you?

01:35:07.100 --> 01:35:08.660
- Nice to finally,

01:35:08.660 --> 01:35:10.690
which I know the last
questioner brought that up,

01:35:10.690 --> 01:35:11.930
beauty of the virtual world.

01:35:11.930 --> 01:35:14.550
I finally-
- Yes.

01:35:14.550 --> 01:35:15.973
Fantastic, yeah.

01:35:17.127 --> 01:35:19.730
Tristan and I go way back. (laughing)

01:35:19.730 --> 01:35:21.793
- One of my big influences.

01:35:23.179 --> 01:35:26.270
So, but I'll be, try
my best to be concise.

01:35:26.270 --> 01:35:30.210
So I have two interconnected questions.

01:35:30.210 --> 01:35:32.520
Do you see, 'cause you
know when we have that,

01:35:32.520 --> 01:35:34.740
you know, that issue with
the race, ethnicity thing

01:35:34.740 --> 01:35:37.810
and I've sort of made the
same observations you have

01:35:37.810 --> 01:35:39.570
within, you know, Egyptology

01:35:39.570 --> 01:35:41.280
and, you know, study,

01:35:41.280 --> 01:35:43.280
you know, in classics.

01:35:43.280 --> 01:35:47.120
Do you think also that
there's a relationship

01:35:47.120 --> 01:35:51.150
between culture and race or an overlap?

01:35:51.150 --> 01:35:53.790
And the second thing
that's intertwined to that,

01:35:53.790 --> 01:35:55.473
do you think, keep in mind,

01:35:56.380 --> 01:35:58.400
and I use race and racism distinctively,

01:35:58.400 --> 01:35:59.840
I make that distinction,

01:35:59.840 --> 01:36:04.220
do you think there's
with Roman imperialism

01:36:04.220 --> 01:36:07.440
that you can make an argument for racism,

01:36:07.440 --> 01:36:09.770
you know, during the Roman Empire

01:36:09.770 --> 01:36:11.620
or as a basis of the Roman Empire?

01:36:11.620 --> 01:36:12.880
I hope my question makes sense.

01:36:12.880 --> 01:36:15.940
And that's my two questions.

01:36:15.940 --> 01:36:16.773
- Yeah,

01:36:17.620 --> 01:36:18.453
okay.

01:36:18.453 --> 01:36:20.803
So let me answer the second one first.

01:36:24.305 --> 01:36:25.138
Well, let me ask,

01:36:26.030 --> 01:36:28.360
oh, nevermind. (laughing)

01:36:28.360 --> 01:36:31.793
I really struggle with this
question about race and culture.

01:36:33.430 --> 01:36:34.550
Right?

01:36:34.550 --> 01:36:37.913
Is it like chicken and egg or is it?

01:36:42.560 --> 01:36:43.393
I think,

01:36:49.743 --> 01:36:51.560
what was the number one way,

01:36:51.560 --> 01:36:53.730
and whether we're talking about Rome

01:36:53.730 --> 01:36:56.500
or whether we're talking about, you know,

01:36:56.500 --> 01:36:58.260
ancient Hellenic societies

01:36:58.260 --> 01:37:00.280
or we're talking about

01:37:01.510 --> 01:37:03.683
modern day, I guess, right?

01:37:04.550 --> 01:37:09.153
What's the first thing you
have to do to make a slave?

01:37:14.730 --> 01:37:15.563
Right?

01:37:15.563 --> 01:37:17.573
But what's the first one you have to do.

01:37:18.840 --> 01:37:20.770
Nobody wants to think about this.

01:37:20.770 --> 01:37:22.523
Nobody wants to think about this.

01:37:23.930 --> 01:37:26.283
You strip them of their culture.

01:37:27.530 --> 01:37:30.480
You take them away from their culture.

01:37:30.480 --> 01:37:31.670
Right?

01:37:31.670 --> 01:37:36.670
So that would make me
think then if that's true

01:37:36.960 --> 01:37:41.610
and this was happening before
there was color slavery.

01:37:41.610 --> 01:37:43.003
Right, right?

01:37:43.950 --> 01:37:47.710
Then there's culture

01:37:47.710 --> 01:37:48.543
and then

01:37:50.040 --> 01:37:53.520
later at some point, right,

01:37:53.520 --> 01:37:56.830
or we can call culture, race, right,

01:37:56.830 --> 01:38:01.830
if we're talking about sort of
pre-transatlantic slave trade

01:38:02.070 --> 01:38:03.933
or pre-color slavery,

01:38:05.340 --> 01:38:07.860
and really I'm getting
out of my wheelhouse here,

01:38:07.860 --> 01:38:09.430
so bear with me.

01:38:09.430 --> 01:38:14.280
But so you strip someone
of their culture, right,

01:38:14.280 --> 01:38:16.080
to dehumanize them,

01:38:16.080 --> 01:38:16.913
to,

01:38:18.060 --> 01:38:18.893
right,

01:38:18.893 --> 01:38:21.653
disassociate them of
what gives them power.

01:38:22.500 --> 01:38:23.333
Right?

01:38:25.278 --> 01:38:27.080
So in that way,

01:38:27.080 --> 01:38:30.963
I mean, I think you could say
they're synonymous, right?

01:38:32.520 --> 01:38:33.663
So,

01:38:35.021 --> 01:38:36.310
and a part of that is, you know,

01:38:36.310 --> 01:38:39.724
not letting them have
their own language, right?

01:38:39.724 --> 01:38:40.557
Right?

01:38:40.557 --> 01:38:42.703
People that you want to enslave,

01:38:43.600 --> 01:38:46.343
you take away their,

01:38:48.960 --> 01:38:51.550
the way they express themselves,

01:38:51.550 --> 01:38:53.280
you take away their language,

01:38:53.280 --> 01:38:56.324
you take away their religion

01:38:56.324 --> 01:38:57.157
or the religious practices.

01:38:59.570 --> 01:39:01.300
You, right,

01:39:01.300 --> 01:39:04.510
you stifle their brains

01:39:04.510 --> 01:39:06.913
by not allowing them to be educated.

01:39:07.860 --> 01:39:08.773
So,

01:39:10.030 --> 01:39:10.863
yeah.

01:39:11.920 --> 01:39:15.305
Now that happened, right,

01:39:15.305 --> 01:39:18.940
look what the Spartans
did to the Helots, right?

01:39:20.140 --> 01:39:22.340
That happened in the ancient,

